WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:13.610 --> 00:00:18.450
Tim Ferriss: Hello, boys and girls, ladies
and germs. This is Tim Ferriss. Welcome to

00:00:18.450 --> 00:00:23.080
another episode of The Tim Ferriss Show. We're
getting close to 700 episodes. Crazy to think.

00:00:23.080 --> 00:00:26.500
And my guest today, long overdue, is Jason
Calacanis.

00:00:26.500 --> 00:00:30.460
Jason Calacanis: Calacanis. There it is.
Tim Ferriss: Don't leave out the second A,

00:00:30.460 --> 00:00:35.390
folks. He has invested in more than 300 startups
in the past decade, who is Sequoia Capital's

00:00:35.390 --> 00:00:39.809
first scout and is the author of the book
Angel. He also hosts two podcasts, we're going

00:00:39.809 --> 00:00:45.910
to talk quite a bit about, This Week in Startups
and All-In. You can find them online at Calacanis,

00:00:45.910 --> 00:00:51.910
that's C-A-L-A-C-A-N-I-S.com and on Twitter
@Jason, because he was an early bird on the

00:00:51.910 --> 00:00:53.200
Twitter platform. Jason, nice to see you,
man.

00:00:53.200 --> 00:01:00.079
Jason Calacanis: It's nice to see you, brother.
You and I have random phone calls for an hour

00:01:00.079 --> 00:01:03.519
or two and I think this is the first time
we're actually recording it.

00:01:03.519 --> 00:01:04.620
Tim Ferriss: First time.
Jason Calacanis: The first time we're actually

00:01:04.620 --> 00:01:08.299
recording it, which is great. I listened to,
I would say, two out of three of your episodes.

00:01:08.299 --> 00:01:10.590
You've had a lot of investors on.
Tim Ferriss: I have.

00:01:10.590 --> 00:01:13.390
Jason Calacanis: Somebody's like, "How come
you haven't been on Tim Ferriss' Podcast?

00:01:13.390 --> 00:01:18.830
I'm like, "He's got so many investors on,
what can I add?" I'm sure at some point —

00:01:18.830 --> 00:01:22.009
Tim Ferriss: There's a lot.
Jason Calacanis: I'm sure. But it is so great

00:01:22.009 --> 00:01:24.440
to see how well your podcast has done over
the years.

00:01:24.440 --> 00:01:27.299
Tim Ferriss: Oh, thanks man.
Jason Calacanis: I remember the first time

00:01:27.299 --> 00:01:32.590
I met you in Tahoe with, I guess Sacca. He
was like, "Hey, this is The 4-hour Workweek

00:01:32.590 --> 00:01:36.860
guy." And I was like, "Do you know how hard
you're making my life? All these founders?

00:01:36.860 --> 00:01:40.770
Now they think I work four hours." I was breaking
your chops.

00:01:40.770 --> 00:01:44.660
Tim Ferriss: It's part of the charm. It's
part of the charm.

00:01:44.660 --> 00:01:49.230
Jason Calacanis: Good to see you.
Tim Ferriss: It is good to see you. You, I

00:01:49.230 --> 00:01:54.110
consider a master of podcasting. We're going
to come back to that, because I have some

00:01:54.110 --> 00:01:59.320
other questions maybe before that. But I wanted
to give an example of a great piece of tactical

00:01:59.320 --> 00:02:05.619
advice. And you actually told me about this
before we started recording. QuickTime. So

00:02:05.619 --> 00:02:11.420
we are both running backup QuickTime audio,
so we have extra local copies in case something

00:02:11.420 --> 00:02:17.410
breaks, because things often break. What was
the tactic that you shared? Because it's so

00:02:17.410 --> 00:02:19.330
smart.
Jason Calacanis: You load a QuickTime thing

00:02:19.330 --> 00:02:22.690
and you're like, "Hey, can you start a QuickTime?"
People go, "Oh yeah. My QuickTime's running."

00:02:22.690 --> 00:02:25.650
But then one out of three times, at the end
of the show we're like, "Okay, can you upload

00:02:25.650 --> 00:02:29.150
the QuickTime?" They're like, "Oh, I didn't
hit the record button." So instead of asking,

00:02:29.150 --> 00:02:34.270
"Can you record a QuickTime?" I say, "How
many seconds is your QuickTime running?" And

00:02:34.270 --> 00:02:39.120
they say, "It's been 36 seconds." Now I know
it's running. But the bigger piece is there's

00:02:39.120 --> 00:02:43.720
this book, The Checklist Manifesto, you and
I are obsessed with, probably part of our

00:02:43.720 --> 00:02:45.140
kinship.
Tim Ferriss: Atul Gawande.

00:02:45.140 --> 00:02:49.520
Jason Calacanis: Yeah. We're both into tools
and optimization. And this book, I had heard

00:02:49.520 --> 00:02:53.880
Jack from Twitter would give it to every Square
employee, give it to every Twitter employee,

00:02:53.880 --> 00:03:00.580
The Checklist Manifesto. And I make everybody
read it, I'm obsessed with checklists, because

00:03:00.580 --> 00:03:06.670
people forget. And all those great stories
in the book about surgeons and pilots were

00:03:06.670 --> 00:03:12.470
like, "I don't need a checklist." And then
everybody dies. And then a nurse comes along

00:03:12.470 --> 00:03:16.410
and was like, "I could just make this little
checklist for you and you say something. I

00:03:16.410 --> 00:03:19.940
say, 'Okay.' You say something. I say, 'Okay.'"
And then all of a sudden you could have a

00:03:19.940 --> 00:03:23.360
plane with four engines because people had
more discipline.

00:03:23.360 --> 00:03:26.300
Tim Ferriss: And it's like, "Oh, I know you
guys didn't need this checklist, but we have

00:03:26.300 --> 00:03:32.110
bacterial line infections down by 39 percent
this year." Go look at that.

00:03:32.110 --> 00:03:37.800
Jason Calacanis: People would take a scalpel
that was dirty and they made this little device,

00:03:37.800 --> 00:03:44.290
these little life hacks that are so much a
part of what you do. And by the way, super

00:03:44.290 --> 00:03:47.450
weird for you to tell me that I'm a podcast
master because I take notes on your podcast.

00:03:47.450 --> 00:03:48.840
Anyway.
Tim Ferriss: It works both ways.

00:03:48.840 --> 00:03:55.370
Jason Calacanis: They would put a little tent
on top of the clean instrument, so a clean

00:03:55.370 --> 00:03:59.890
scalpel would have a tent on top. And the
nurse was responsible for cleaning everything

00:03:59.890 --> 00:04:05.799
and putting the tents on, so then they knew
it was clean. And you take the tent off, now

00:04:05.799 --> 00:04:09.740
it's been used. So "Tent on" means "Clean,
don't put the tent on a dirty scalpel."

00:04:09.740 --> 00:04:12.470
Tim Ferriss: Tent off, no bueno.
Jason Calacanis: No bueno.

00:04:12.470 --> 00:04:15.799
Tim Ferriss: Now, you can't believe everything
you read on the internet, so please correct

00:04:15.799 --> 00:04:22.510
me if I'm getting any of this wrong, but either
your mom was tending to people in the ER,

00:04:22.510 --> 00:04:27.470
your dad had a bar, as I understand it. There
were times when you would be cleaning blood

00:04:27.470 --> 00:04:31.300
off the floor from all the brawls and fights.
Jason Calacanis: That's a true story.

00:04:31.300 --> 00:04:35.790
Tim Ferriss: And then raided at one point,
I think, by the, I don't know if it was the

00:04:35.790 --> 00:04:37.710
FBI, but with —
Jason Calacanis: Tax authorities.

00:04:37.710 --> 00:04:40.600
Tim Ferriss: Tax authorities.
Jason Calacanis: My dad got a little behind

00:04:40.600 --> 00:04:43.930
on the taxes.
Tim Ferriss: Yeah. The taxes, they sneak up

00:04:43.930 --> 00:04:45.740
on you.
Jason Calacanis: It turns out the tax authority,

00:04:45.740 --> 00:04:51.759
they're humorless. You pay your taxes or you
go to jail. And so when I was 18 years old,

00:04:51.759 --> 00:04:56.020
growing up in Bay Ridge, Brooklyn, which is
the last stop on the train, just to give you

00:04:56.020 --> 00:05:01.759
an idea of how far out there it is. This is
the '70s and '80s. In 1988, my dad's bar,

00:05:01.759 --> 00:05:07.500
after the stock market crashed, he went into
basically debt and it was really gnarly. And

00:05:07.500 --> 00:05:13.550
then one summer afternoon in July, the feds
basically and the tax authority came and padlocked

00:05:13.550 --> 00:05:19.680
the place, took everything out of it, and
this close to my dad going to jail, and had

00:05:19.680 --> 00:05:22.920
one of the great traumas of my life.
And that is one of the things I really appreciate

00:05:22.920 --> 00:05:27.400
and respect about you is you shared your trauma
on this podcast. It's like a gift to the world.

00:05:27.400 --> 00:05:31.980
That's a pretty scary thing when you're 17
years old and your dad says, "I'm sorry, son.

00:05:31.980 --> 00:05:36.380
I don't have money for college, I'm probably
going to jail. I tried to make this business

00:05:36.380 --> 00:05:44.440
work, it didn't work. Take care of your mom."
Whoa. It's like that scene when, if you remember

00:05:44.440 --> 00:05:49.330
in The Empire Strikes Back and Han Solo comes
over to Chewbacca, he's like, "Just take care

00:05:49.330 --> 00:05:53.569
of the Princess. I'll be back." It was really
intense. Really intense.

00:05:53.569 --> 00:05:59.810
Tim Ferriss: Yeah. Yeah, I bet that's —
Jason Calacanis: Crazy. My life just got crazier

00:05:59.810 --> 00:06:06.910
and crazier after that. So I forged such a
crazy childhood that was so violent and crazy

00:06:06.910 --> 00:06:14.220
and just Brooklyn, when it wasn't cool, that
it made when I went into business, I was like,

00:06:14.220 --> 00:06:19.860
"I don't have to worry about somebody jumping
me? There's no violence here? I'm not going

00:06:19.860 --> 00:06:24.300
to be murdered? I'm not going to jail?" It's
like, "Wow, this is easier." But I didn't

00:06:24.300 --> 00:06:30.480
know that world existed. The only world I
knew was the people who hung out at my dad's

00:06:30.480 --> 00:06:35.990
bar, the Mafia, the Hell's Angels, a bunch
of cops, some percentage of which were in

00:06:35.990 --> 00:06:41.970
on the take with the other two groups, and
then a bunch of Wall Street people. And so

00:06:41.970 --> 00:06:48.940
it was like out of a film like Goodfellas.
Tim, I kid you not. I would have to go serve

00:06:48.940 --> 00:06:57.650
espresso to the bookie or the dealer or the
head of the Hells Angels. It was bonkers.

00:06:57.650 --> 00:07:01.770
It just made me really good at dealing with
people, I think. And having a certain —

00:07:01.770 --> 00:07:04.710
Tim Ferriss: I would imagine you developed
a lot of situational awareness.

00:07:04.710 --> 00:07:09.129
Jason Calacanis: Adaptations, right? You get
a lot of adaptations real quick when you're

00:07:09.129 --> 00:07:13.770
dealing with those different types of individuals.
But I look back on it now and I'm like, "Did

00:07:13.770 --> 00:07:17.919
that really happen?" And then I look back
at what's happening in my life now, and I'm

00:07:17.919 --> 00:07:20.990
like, "Is this really happening?" So it's
been a pretty surreal life the whole way,

00:07:20.990 --> 00:07:23.930
I have to say.
Tim Ferriss: If we flash forward a little

00:07:23.930 --> 00:07:28.930
bit, and we don't necessarily have to spend
a ton of time on this, but Silicon Alley Reporter

00:07:28.930 --> 00:07:33.240
in the beginning, you're photocopying it yourself,
you're hand-delivering it to coffee shops

00:07:33.240 --> 00:07:39.639
and other spots. Did you know or decide you
were going into business for yourself to be

00:07:39.639 --> 00:07:46.330
an entrepreneur? And I ask especially given
what you saw happen to your dad. I can see

00:07:46.330 --> 00:07:50.669
some people responding by going entirely in
the opposite direction, getting the safest

00:07:50.669 --> 00:07:56.159
nine-to-five possible. So how did you end
up an entrepreneur?

00:07:56.159 --> 00:08:02.360
Jason Calacanis: Having a dad who was an entrepreneur
puts it in you. And I had one or two jobs

00:08:02.360 --> 00:08:07.660
when I was in college. I went to college at
night. So I would fix laser printers and build

00:08:07.660 --> 00:08:11.930
networks. This is before the internet existed,
you would have to connect computers together

00:08:11.930 --> 00:08:16.069
with coaxial cables and create what's called
a local area network. And I did a couple of

00:08:16.069 --> 00:08:18.669
those jobs and it was well-paying and it helped
me pay for college, because remember my dad

00:08:18.669 --> 00:08:21.229
had no money, so I had to pay for all this
on my own. It took me five years to go through

00:08:21.229 --> 00:08:29.740
school at night. But when I would ride the
train in, I would look at people reading magazines

00:08:29.740 --> 00:08:34.720
and then I would go to cafes, pre the web
and mobile phones. And we would go hang out

00:08:34.720 --> 00:08:38.760
in cafes in the Lower East Side, Bleecker
Street, wherever, the Village. When I was

00:08:38.760 --> 00:08:43.880
at college, we'd go play chess on Bleecker
Street or read magazines or free newspapers.

00:08:43.880 --> 00:08:48.790
And I just looked at the people who were in
the pictures, Tim. And I was like, "Why are

00:08:48.790 --> 00:08:52.220
they on the cover of the magazine? Why is
this person on the cover of Spy magazine?

00:08:52.220 --> 00:08:58.480
Why is this person on the cover of Paper magazine?
Why are people important?" All I knew was

00:08:58.480 --> 00:09:03.120
nurses, doctors, lawyers, cops, firefighters,
that's all I was exposed to. I didn't understand

00:09:03.120 --> 00:09:07.460
there was other stuff in the world. Then I
went to school in Manhattan at night and I

00:09:07.460 --> 00:09:11.711
would work in Manhattan during the day, and
I was looking around going, "Who's that person

00:09:11.711 --> 00:09:16.800
getting out of that limousine to go into that
big, tall building? What's in that big tall

00:09:16.800 --> 00:09:20.310
— oh, those are apartments?" It was really
weird, like being dropped on an alien planet

00:09:20.310 --> 00:09:22.320
because Brooklyn was so different than Manhattan
at the time.

00:09:22.320 --> 00:09:24.190
Tim Ferriss: For sure.
Jason Calacanis: Brooklyn was a bunch of blue

00:09:24.190 --> 00:09:28.570
collar people, Manhattan were rich people
and powerful people. And so something just

00:09:28.570 --> 00:09:33.154
clicked in my brain and I said, "I want to
be powerful, I want to be rich, I want to

00:09:33.154 --> 00:09:37.000
be on the cover of a magazine." And I started
looking at the magazines and then I discovered

00:09:37.000 --> 00:09:42.130
the masthead. And I saw the masthead and I
said, "Wait a second. Who's the publisher

00:09:42.130 --> 00:09:47.440
and the editor-in-chief? Who has more power?
Who is this editor-at-large? That sounds more

00:09:47.440 --> 00:09:50.810
important. But wait, it's in a smaller font
and lower." And then I started to meet people

00:09:50.810 --> 00:09:55.490
and I started asking them, "What's this? What's
that?" And I was at a party and I ran into

00:09:55.490 --> 00:09:57.950
somebody at Barneys. Somebody had invited
me to a party in the basement of Barneys.

00:09:57.950 --> 00:10:03.089
And I'm talking to this guy and I said, "What
do you do?" And he says, "I work at a magazine."

00:10:03.089 --> 00:10:04.779
I said, "What magazine do you work at?" He
said, "Paper…"

00:10:04.779 --> 00:10:08.790
Tim Ferriss: Let me pause for a second. Barneys
sounds fancy. How did you get invited to the

00:10:08.790 --> 00:10:10.649
party?
Jason Calacanis: You know what? I just was

00:10:10.649 --> 00:10:16.560
hanging out in the downtown scene where a
company called Voyager was making CD-ROMs,

00:10:16.560 --> 00:10:20.130
Blender was making CD-ROMs, a friend of mine,
Josh Harris, was doing Pseudo and he was doing

00:10:20.130 --> 00:10:24.340
online chat rooms with Prodigy. So I was just
hanging around with the tech scene there,

00:10:24.340 --> 00:10:27.670
and it was probably 50 people who were in
the tech scene. Because the only thing that

00:10:27.670 --> 00:10:33.740
existed were online services, dial-up, CompuServe,
Prodigy, The WELL. It was just a small set

00:10:33.740 --> 00:10:36.990
of dial-up services and I knew how to use
a modem and I knew about the internet. So

00:10:36.990 --> 00:10:41.510
started to meet those people, and then those
people started to tip over into the media

00:10:41.510 --> 00:10:47.980
business in New York, because the media business
was very attracted to, "You can dial up and

00:10:47.980 --> 00:10:52.820
read an article?" People started to put these
connections together, magazines plus a dial-up

00:10:52.820 --> 00:10:58.680
service, music plus a dial-up service, booking
a ticket to a movie, a dial-up service. And

00:10:58.680 --> 00:11:04.860
a CD-ROM has a video on it or it has an article,
so you could make a CD-ROM with an article

00:11:04.860 --> 00:11:07.269
end.
So this multimedia thing happened and then

00:11:07.269 --> 00:11:12.170
I was at Sony setting up their computer networks
when the internet happened. And then Sony

00:11:12.170 --> 00:11:16.899
was going to build a website and so I was
on the website team. And the website was a

00:11:16.899 --> 00:11:23.190
picture of 10 Sony logos, Sony Music, Columbia
Records. We literally sat 10 people in a room

00:11:23.190 --> 00:11:27.350
designing a webpage with 10 logos on it. That
was the entirety of the website, it was one

00:11:27.350 --> 00:11:33.050
page, and then a 12-page legal disclaimer.
So this was a very interesting moment in time.

00:11:33.050 --> 00:11:38.339
And I said, "What magazine?" He said, "Paper
magazine." I said, "You work at Paper magazine?"

00:11:38.339 --> 00:11:42.950
And he said, "I created Paper magazine." I
said, "What do you mean you created it? You

00:11:42.950 --> 00:11:46.450
work at the printer?" He said, "No, no. I'm
the editor-in-chief. I'm David Hershkovits."

00:11:46.450 --> 00:11:50.440
I said, "Tell me everything." So I start talking
to him. He says, "Why don't you come by my

00:11:50.440 --> 00:11:56.470
office, because I'm setting up my dial-up."
And I said, "Okay." And I go down to his office,

00:11:56.470 --> 00:12:02.670
which was where Balthazar is now at Lower
Broadway. And I help him with his internet,

00:12:02.670 --> 00:12:05.550
and we're talking about dial-up services.
"You should write a column for us about the

00:12:05.550 --> 00:12:09.750
internet and this technology stuff." So I
started writing for Paper magazine. And I

00:12:09.750 --> 00:12:12.089
was this little nerdy kid. I didn't know how
to write, I didn't know how to spell, I didn't

00:12:12.089 --> 00:12:18.639
know how to put a comma in a sentence.
So Christine Muhlke was my editor. She was

00:12:18.639 --> 00:12:21.820
20-something years old, I was 20-something
years old. I started writing for Paper magazine.

00:12:21.820 --> 00:12:26.220
And then Hershkovits, he was really getting
into the internet. And I remain friends with

00:12:26.220 --> 00:12:30.010
him to this day, 30 years later. And he would
just ask me to come by the office to hang

00:12:30.010 --> 00:12:34.140
out. I'm hanging at Paper magazine and he's
like, "Hey, you want to go to a concert tonight?"

00:12:34.140 --> 00:12:40.630
"Yeah, sure." He says, "We're going to go
to the Roxy. This new band, Chemical Brothers

00:12:40.630 --> 00:12:43.030
is going to be playing. Orb is going to be
opening for them."

00:12:43.030 --> 00:12:48.139
And then we're in the green room or the VIP
room waiting to go on and he's like, "Jason,

00:12:48.139 --> 00:12:52.510
meet this woman from Iceland." And I'm like,
"You're from Iceland. Where is that? Is that

00:12:52.510 --> 00:12:57.430
Norway or Sweden?" And she's like, "No, it's
over here." And just talking really low. And

00:12:57.430 --> 00:13:00.170
I'm like, "So what do you do?" She's like,
"I have a record coming out." And I'm like,

00:13:00.170 --> 00:13:05.130
"Oh, that's awesome. What kind of music? Is
it like these guys?" She says, "No, just I'm

00:13:05.130 --> 00:13:08.029
a singer." I said, "I'm Jason." And she goes,
"I'm Björk." This is New York in the '90s.

00:13:08.029 --> 00:13:15.720
It was cool as F. It was the zeitgeist was
crazy. So all of this starts to spiral and

00:13:15.720 --> 00:13:19.890
I just said, "I need to make a magazine."
And David had showed me the early issues of

00:13:19.890 --> 00:13:23.339
Paper. And Paper magazine was originally —
Tim Ferriss: I've never heard you censor your

00:13:23.339 --> 00:13:25.589
cursing before. That was very delicate of
you.

00:13:25.589 --> 00:13:28.889
Jason Calacanis: It's a family show, I think.
Tim Ferriss: You can curse. It's okay.

00:13:28.889 --> 00:13:33.699
Jason Calacanis: Don't tell a kid from Brooklyn
to start cursing. Your poor editors are going

00:13:33.699 --> 00:13:35.920
to be — you just created three hours of
editing.

00:13:35.920 --> 00:13:39.810
Tim Ferriss: Oh, boy. Search, replace. We
have 47 fucks in the first 30 minutes. All

00:13:39.810 --> 00:13:42.929
right. Good.
Jason Calacanis: 47 fucks given. So anyway,

00:13:42.929 --> 00:13:47.830
it's actually fun to remember all this stuff.
So he takes me to the archive room of Paper

00:13:47.830 --> 00:13:51.769
magazine, which was a glossy at the time.
And he says, "Look." It was a foldable thing.

00:13:51.769 --> 00:13:56.000
I got a foldable piece of paper and I folded
it and we would just put it for free in cafes.

00:13:56.000 --> 00:14:05.279
You know in The Terminator, you see from his
view and all the words are going by and he's

00:14:05.279 --> 00:14:09.080
just processing everything really fast, that
was happening in my brain. I was like, "Okay,

00:14:09.080 --> 00:14:14.820
wait. Free, fold it, print it. What? Okay.
Do this." And I'm taking all of these instructions.

00:14:14.820 --> 00:14:18.430
And I literally went to Tower Records, which
is a place where they used to sell CDs and

00:14:18.430 --> 00:14:23.330
records and they had a zine section. And zines
were short for magazines, Z-I-N-E, for people

00:14:23.330 --> 00:14:26.740
who are under the age of 40.
And this was the most punk rock thing you

00:14:26.740 --> 00:14:31.320
could do, after being in a punk rock band,
was to start a zine. And a zine just meant

00:14:31.320 --> 00:14:35.450
you got your friends together and wrote an
article together and then you would photocopy

00:14:35.450 --> 00:14:39.880
it. So 2600 famously was a zine about hacking
in New York. And I had met the founder of

00:14:39.880 --> 00:14:45.199
that. So there's a little zine culture going
on and I was like, "Huh. All my friends are

00:14:45.199 --> 00:14:49.990
starting the internet stuff, I'm writing about
the internet once a month, I write 400 words,

00:14:49.990 --> 00:14:54.639
I could write 40,000 words. I can write 4,000
words, whatever." And people had never been

00:14:54.639 --> 00:14:59.310
to San Francisco or Silicon Valley, but somebody
referred to what we were doing as Silicon

00:14:59.310 --> 00:15:08.949
Alley. And I was like, "Silicon Alley? That's
rad." And then I was like, "I should be a

00:15:08.949 --> 00:15:13.680
reporter about Silicon Alley. I could do a
Silicon Alley Reporter and it would just be

00:15:13.680 --> 00:15:19.690
me reporting on what's happening here because
this is cool. Now, it's 200 people doing stuff."

00:15:19.690 --> 00:15:23.650
And so I just started a photocopy called Silicon
Alley Reporter. And I went up to the Village

00:15:23.650 --> 00:15:30.300
Printing Shop on 43rd Street and Sixth Avenue.
And every time I'm in New York, I like to

00:15:30.300 --> 00:15:34.339
walk when I'm in New York just to remind myself
of those moments in time. And I always walk

00:15:34.339 --> 00:15:38.300
down that block to see if it's there. Village
Printer, I know it's still there, a couple

00:15:38.300 --> 00:15:43.360
years ago it's still there. And it was 24
hours. And I brought it to the guy and there's

00:15:43.360 --> 00:15:47.810
a thing called tabloid paper. Eight and a
half by 11 plus eight and a half by 11 equals

00:15:47.810 --> 00:15:53.180
whatever it is. The 17 by whatever.
And you could take that paper, fold it, and

00:15:53.180 --> 00:15:57.420
put a staple in the middle and it would look
like a newsletter. And so I said, "Okay, that's

00:15:57.420 --> 00:16:01.750
the format." I got PageMaker, I started laying
it out, I took pictures, I wrote a couple

00:16:01.750 --> 00:16:05.210
articles, and I went there and I printed it
and he said, "It's going to be 10 cents a

00:16:05.210 --> 00:16:10.800
photocopy." And I was like, "All right. 80
cents an issue, I could do this." So I go,

00:16:10.800 --> 00:16:15.790
I said, "Make me a 1,000 copies, 800 bucks,
right?" And I'm just going to start handing

00:16:15.790 --> 00:16:19.149
them out like David Hershkovits did. And I
go and he hands me a bill for 1,750. And I

00:16:19.149 --> 00:16:23.430
said, "Wait, you said 10 cents. It's eight
pages." He said, "It's double-sided." I said,

00:16:23.430 --> 00:16:28.339
"It's double-sided." I said, "I don't have
the money." He goes, "What do you mean you

00:16:28.339 --> 00:16:31.860
don't have the money? I got a thousand copies
of Silicon Alley Reporter here."

00:16:31.860 --> 00:16:35.500
And so I start talking to the guy. Now I'm
haggling with him. And he's just the overnight

00:16:35.500 --> 00:16:39.880
manager. And I said, "Listen, I don't have
the money for this. My credit card's maxed

00:16:39.880 --> 00:16:43.730
out. Can you do me a favor? Maybe someday
I could do you a favor. I could put an ad

00:16:43.730 --> 00:16:47.880
in there." And he's like, "Don't tell anybody,
okay?" And I was like, "No, I won't tell anybody."

00:16:47.880 --> 00:16:52.399
He's like, "They got a counter on these things.
I could get busted." And I was like, "I don't

00:16:52.399 --> 00:16:56.440
want you to get busted." He's like, "I don't
know, man. This thing's really cool. Just

00:16:56.440 --> 00:17:00.069
give me 800 bucks." And I just gave him 800
bucks.

00:17:00.069 --> 00:17:06.689
Then I realized, I started to learn a very
interesting lesson in my life that when I

00:17:06.689 --> 00:17:13.920
ask people things, that many times people
will do what I ask them to do, like a Jedi

00:17:13.920 --> 00:17:18.589
kind of trick, but this guy did it out the
kindness of his soul. But I started to learn

00:17:18.589 --> 00:17:24.770
early in my career, you could ask for things
in the world, even sometimes outrageous things,

00:17:24.770 --> 00:17:30.710
and sometimes it happens. And I took them
over to Roseland where there was a internet

00:17:30.710 --> 00:17:35.660
party going on for — Roseland's a venue,
a music venue. And then some electronic music

00:17:35.660 --> 00:17:39.400
person had played and then a bunch of internet
multimedia people were going to be hanging

00:17:39.400 --> 00:17:42.270
out.
So I walk over there, I had dropped off mostly

00:17:42.270 --> 00:17:47.600
issues at my little office I had. A little
office-share. And then I took 200 of them

00:17:47.600 --> 00:17:52.260
in my backpack and in my hands to the party.
I get to the party at 1:00 in the morning,

00:17:52.260 --> 00:17:59.020
and I just start handing them to people. And
I had this moment of clarity in my life. People

00:17:59.020 --> 00:18:02.690
started coming up to me to get them. And then
I turn around and I look at the party, which

00:18:02.690 --> 00:18:09.410
was popping off. And every single person has
the magazine open, the zine open, the photocopy,

00:18:09.410 --> 00:18:13.059
and is reading it with two or three people
around them. The entire party stopped.

00:18:13.059 --> 00:18:17.850
Tim Ferriss: So what did that feel like? What
happened to you when you saw that?

00:18:17.850 --> 00:18:20.070
Jason Calacanis: Power.
Tim Ferriss: Oh, okay. Tell me more.

00:18:20.070 --> 00:18:22.560
Jason Calacanis: I got the power.
Tim Ferriss: You got the power. Tell me more.

00:18:22.560 --> 00:18:25.970
Jason Calacanis: Remember I said I had no
power as a child, and they take your dad's

00:18:25.970 --> 00:18:31.870
bar? We're going deep into the trauma work
here. Listen, I consider it a privilege to

00:18:31.870 --> 00:18:35.250
be on here and you've shared a lot and if
this helps other people, but when you don't

00:18:35.250 --> 00:18:39.960
have power and you're a kid and you live a
scared life, which I did, and then all of

00:18:39.960 --> 00:18:48.690
a sudden the entire party in Manhattan is
in the palm of your hand and something you

00:18:48.690 --> 00:18:55.750
created has stopped everybody dead in their
tracks, the adrenaline and the confidence,

00:18:55.750 --> 00:19:02.720
it felt like all of a sudden Superman figures
out when he reaches into the fire to get something

00:19:02.720 --> 00:19:08.830
he dropped and it's like, "Oh, the fire doesn't
burn me? Whoa. I can fly?" Just these incredible

00:19:08.830 --> 00:19:13.280
wings sprouted. And I was like, "Wait a second.
I could be an entrepreneur. I could do something

00:19:13.280 --> 00:19:18.929
in the world."
And the next day I was at it. My phone was

00:19:18.929 --> 00:19:23.890
ringing, people were calling me. And then
I just made a set of postcards that said,

00:19:23.890 --> 00:19:29.900
"Subscribe to The Silicon Alley Reporter.
10 issues a year, $90." And I had seen Esther

00:19:29.900 --> 00:19:33.500
Dyson charged $1,000 for her newsletter. So
I was like, "She's Esther Dyson. So if I charged

00:19:33.500 --> 00:19:40.630
10 percent, maybe that's the same thing."
Esther Dyson was this famous Angel investor.

00:19:40.630 --> 00:19:44.159
When I went out, if I didn't have the magazine
on me, I had the cards and I'd just hand the

00:19:44.159 --> 00:19:48.210
cards. And then within two or three months
I would get to my office-share and there would

00:19:48.210 --> 00:19:54.030
be a stack of envelopes and it would be 20
people sent me $90. It's $1,800.

00:19:54.030 --> 00:19:57.020
Tim Ferriss: Mailbox money. What a feeling.
Jason Calacanis: Every day, $4000, $6,000.

00:19:57.020 --> 00:20:05.559
And all of a sudden there was 30, $40,000,
people subscribing. And it was like the Kevin

00:20:05.559 --> 00:20:10.010
Kelly, "You only need 2,000 true fans." And
then all of a sudden I was like, "Wait a second.

00:20:10.010 --> 00:20:15.659
I can quit my jobs. I can do this. I'm David
Hershkovits and Kim from Paper. I did it."

00:20:15.659 --> 00:20:23.309
And I was like, "Whoa." And this all happened
within 60 days. From a nobody trying to get

00:20:23.309 --> 00:20:28.710
into a party to meet somebody, to all of a
sudden every CEO and person trying to meet

00:20:28.710 --> 00:20:34.440
me. And it was basically, this all of a sudden
becomes the Sliding Doors moment of your life.

00:20:34.440 --> 00:20:37.940
Some random occurrence happened, I meet David
Hershkovits at a party. I get this guy to

00:20:37.940 --> 00:20:43.200
print out the things, I go to that party and
drop them off, and all of a sudden I'm important.

00:20:43.200 --> 00:20:50.120
But remember, I wasn't covering tennis, I
wasn't covering wine, I was covering this

00:20:50.120 --> 00:20:56.220
new thing called the internet when the browser
didn't support graphics. So the timing was

00:20:56.220 --> 00:21:02.130
so insane because the intern — you talked
about this Forrest Gump thing, now that's

00:21:02.130 --> 00:21:07.870
a problem. This is going to stick. Everybody's
going to be like, "I guess life is like a

00:21:07.870 --> 00:21:12.930
box of chocolates, Tim." I just turned into
the Forrest Gump of the internet industry,

00:21:12.930 --> 00:21:17.820
it's going to stick forever. I'll take it.
I'll take it. Forrest Gump had a great life.

00:21:17.820 --> 00:21:20.210
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, yeah. He did. He did.
Jason Calacanis: That was the start of a great

00:21:20.210 --> 00:21:25.960
life for a very simple man.
Tim Ferriss: I'm so glad we're talking about

00:21:25.960 --> 00:21:29.452
the early days because, of course, I had no
idea of a lot of this stuff.

00:21:29.452 --> 00:21:31.480
Jason Calacanis: But you had the same thing.
Nobody knew who you were, you were some guy

00:21:31.480 --> 00:21:36.970
and all of a sudden this 4-Hour Workweek goes
from — you got paid nothing for that book

00:21:36.970 --> 00:21:39.940
and then all of a sudden — wasn't it the
same exact experience?

00:21:39.940 --> 00:21:44.630
Tim Ferriss: It was similar. It was similar.
Yeah. It was very similar. I remember when

00:21:44.630 --> 00:21:50.029
the book came out and I got a call from my
editor, Heather Jackson, at the time. And

00:21:50.029 --> 00:21:55.190
she said, "Why, hello, Mr. New York Times
Bestselling Author." And I had just finished

00:21:55.190 --> 00:22:00.550
a day of radio satellite tours, which people
did back in the day. They may still do them.

00:22:00.550 --> 00:22:05.539
And I'd had two pots of coffee and I was just
exhausted. And I sat down on the floor and

00:22:05.539 --> 00:22:11.630
I said, "Heather, don't fuck with me. I'm
too tired." And she said, "I'm serious. No,

00:22:11.630 --> 00:22:18.710
you made it. You're 15 on the extended list.
Not in print, but you're on the list technically."

00:22:18.710 --> 00:22:23.539
And I thought, thought to myself, "Oh, wow.
Things are about to change. Things are about

00:22:23.539 --> 00:22:27.490
to change."
And all of a sudden, let's call it within

00:22:27.490 --> 00:22:33.120
the next few weeks, it kept growing and then
it stayed on the print list for, I want to

00:22:33.120 --> 00:22:39.140
say, four years plus unbroken. And in the
beginning, I started getting these calls asking

00:22:39.140 --> 00:22:44.409
me to speak, offering to pay me for keynotes.
None of this had happened before and it was

00:22:44.409 --> 00:22:45.409
—
Jason Calacanis: Mind-blowing.

00:22:45.409 --> 00:22:48.871
Tim Ferriss: — bewildering. Yeah. Very bewildering.
Jason Calacanis: The speaking gig is an interesting

00:22:48.871 --> 00:22:54.169
moment because you work your whole life, you
make 30 grand, 40 grand a year, and then this

00:22:54.169 --> 00:22:58.360
kind of stuff happens to you. And then somebody
calls, "Oh, Tim. Would you like to come speak

00:22:58.360 --> 00:23:02.980
at this corporate thing?" And you're like,
"Why would I speak there?" "Oh, well, your

00:23:02.980 --> 00:23:05.610
book." And you're like, "Okay." And they're
like, "Of course, we'd like to give you an

00:23:05.610 --> 00:23:09.680
honorarium." And you're like, "Okay." And
they're like, "And it's $30,000," or "40,000."

00:23:09.680 --> 00:23:14.169
Whatever. And you're like, "That's what I
made last year."

00:23:14.169 --> 00:23:18.510
Tim Ferriss: Come again? Come again?
Jason Calacanis: "$3,000?" "No. Add a zero,

00:23:18.510 --> 00:23:21.320
30,000." And you're just like, "That makes
no sense." And they're like, "It's going to

00:23:21.320 --> 00:23:23.860
be two hours of your time. We don't want to
put you out, Mr. Ferriss." And you're like

00:23:23.860 --> 00:23:30.480
"Can I do it tomorrow too?" It is a mind-blowing
experience. I've literally just had one of

00:23:30.480 --> 00:23:34.850
these come in today, some corporate gig. Because
All-In's gotten so popular, all of a sudden

00:23:34.850 --> 00:23:39.029
now people want me to speak at a bunch of
stuff again. And it's just the numbers are

00:23:39.029 --> 00:23:43.279
mind-boggling. And you're just like, isn't
it very weird how you can go from being an

00:23:43.279 --> 00:23:46.890
absolute nobody to somebody wanting to give
you tens of thousands of dollars to speak

00:23:46.890 --> 00:23:49.919
for an hour? That really screwed with my mind
a lot. Also, because I had a lot of feelings

00:23:49.919 --> 00:23:54.080
of guilt and stuff about my parents. And I
was like, "You're offering me a speaking gig,

00:23:54.080 --> 00:23:58.040
which is what my mom or dad made in a year."
Tim Ferriss: In a year. Yeah. It's weird.

00:23:58.040 --> 00:24:01.400
Jason Calacanis: You also had this —
Tim Ferriss: Same situation. So weird.

00:24:01.400 --> 00:24:04.220
Jason Calacanis: It really does screw with
your head and it's very hard to stay grounded

00:24:04.220 --> 00:24:08.470
or figure out what's actually happening, right?
Tim Ferriss: Yeah. Let's talk about another

00:24:08.470 --> 00:24:14.779
"Holy shit" moment. And this is January of
'99. You're offered —

00:24:14.779 --> 00:24:19.179
Jason Calacanis: You've got all the moments.
Tim Ferriss: — 20 million, I believe.

00:24:19.179 --> 00:24:23.100
Jason Calacanis: It's a true story.
Tim Ferriss: I'm reading here, this is from

00:24:23.100 --> 00:24:26.710
Fast Company and it talks about your interaction
with —

00:24:26.710 --> 00:24:27.840
Jason Calacanis: Alan Meckler.
Tim Ferriss: I think this was with I think

00:24:27.840 --> 00:24:30.830
Douglas Rushkoff.
Jason Calacanis: Oh, Doug Rushkoff. My friend

00:24:30.830 --> 00:24:34.860
in New York. That was another guy who taught
me so much. I love Doug.

00:24:34.860 --> 00:24:40.130
Tim Ferriss: Walk me through getting this
offer, the conversations you had, the decision

00:24:40.130 --> 00:24:47.809
you made, and then if anything you take from
that to this day, if you wouldn't mind.

00:24:47.809 --> 00:24:52.029
Jason Calacanis: The magazine had gotten very
big and it became a color glossy. I had almost

00:24:52.029 --> 00:24:56.760
a hundred people working for me. It was 300
pages and it was during the dot com boom.

00:24:56.760 --> 00:25:01.559
Talking about 1999 now. So the magazine is
doing six, seven, eight million a year. I

00:25:01.559 --> 00:25:04.970
had done an event or two that started making
two or three million. So now I've got an $11

00:25:04.970 --> 00:25:11.659
million company, I built up my credit cards
and I was making, just a couple years earlier,

00:25:11.659 --> 00:25:17.130
minimum wage to work in the computer room
at Fordham, which was $3.50 an hour. And then

00:25:17.130 --> 00:25:22.650
I was fixing laser printers for $9 an hour.
And then I got a job at Amnesty International

00:25:22.650 --> 00:25:27.590
doing their internal network for $12 an hour.
So all of this happens in a very short period

00:25:27.590 --> 00:25:30.460
of time. And again, for a poor kid from Brooklyn,
I'm just trying to navigate it.

00:25:30.460 --> 00:25:33.559
And this guy, Alan Meckler had internet.com,
he took it public, it became worth a billion

00:25:33.559 --> 00:25:38.240
dollars and he offered me 20 million bucks
for Silicon Alley Reporter. But it was my

00:25:38.240 --> 00:25:44.809
identity and it was making 10 million. And
I was like, "I don't want to give up my identity."

00:25:44.809 --> 00:25:49.529
But I thought about it, I said, "If I can
clear a million or $2 million a year off the

00:25:49.529 --> 00:25:52.700
magazine in profits, it'd be the same amount
of money. So maybe I wouldn't do." And then

00:25:52.700 --> 00:25:57.390
the dot com bust happens and I lose everything.
And I wound up selling the scraps of the business

00:25:57.390 --> 00:26:02.919
to Dow Jones. And they give me two years of
salary and the magazine's dead. And I get

00:26:02.919 --> 00:26:10.360
two years at $300,000. It's maybe five or
$600,000 in this employment agreement. And

00:26:10.360 --> 00:26:13.620
they paid off all the bills because I then
had, like my dad, I was in arrears when the

00:26:13.620 --> 00:26:15.580
magazine collapsed.
If you've got a magazine about the internet

00:26:15.580 --> 00:26:17.000
and the internet collapses, literally not
only —

00:26:17.000 --> 00:26:19.110
Tim Ferriss: Tough business.
Jason Calacanis: — not only did I not have

00:26:19.110 --> 00:26:23.850
any money, all the money that was owed to
me never showed up, because all the companies

00:26:23.850 --> 00:26:25.940
had gone out of business. So I was like, "You
owe me for three ads." They're like, "Phone

00:26:25.940 --> 00:26:29.750
number didn't pick up." The company was shut
down. And so then I show up for work at Dow

00:26:29.750 --> 00:26:32.720
Jones and they say, "Hey, can we take you
for a walk?" It's middle manager guy. And

00:26:32.720 --> 00:26:37.740
I'm like, "Sure. Walk? Okay, sure. We'll go
for a walk." We walk. "Hey, listen, you're

00:26:37.740 --> 00:26:44.520
so talented. We don't think — you being
here and you're so talented." And I'm like,

00:26:44.520 --> 00:26:50.390
"Yeah?" And they're like, "So you should go
do something else." And I'm like, "Are you

00:26:50.390 --> 00:26:54.529
firing me?" And they're like, "Yeah, we don't
need your services." I said, "You just bought

00:26:54.529 --> 00:26:59.160
my company and the 20..." I had 20 people
left and I reassigned them to this other venture

00:26:59.160 --> 00:27:04.450
capital thing they were doing.
And I said, "You don't want me, Jason Calacanis,

00:27:04.450 --> 00:27:09.020
the Silicon Alley Reporter, to work here?"
They said, "No." I said, "I just signed a

00:27:09.020 --> 00:27:14.610
contract with you for five or $600,000." They
said, "Yeah." And he pulls out an envelope

00:27:14.610 --> 00:27:21.870
and he hands me a check for $500,000. He says,
"We're paying out your contract." Tim, you're

00:27:21.870 --> 00:27:26.700
the first person I've told these stories to
really. This is literally the crazy life I've

00:27:26.700 --> 00:27:32.770
lived. And I am looking at a $500,000 check,
which is a extraordinary amount of money,

00:27:32.770 --> 00:27:37.000
obviously. And the only thing I can think
is, "I want to beat the shit out of this guy.

00:27:37.000 --> 00:27:44.720
You just fucking fired me? You don't want
me. Who went from being a nobody to running

00:27:44.720 --> 00:27:47.830
New York, being on Charlie Rose, being on
the cover of The New York Times, having a

00:27:47.830 --> 00:27:54.010
New Yorker thing being written about me, just
living the peak New York experience.

00:27:54.010 --> 00:27:57.820
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.
Jason Calacanis: Living in a loft in Manhattan,

00:27:57.820 --> 00:28:02.809
sitting in the first three or four rows of
the Knicks games, hanging out with Allan Houston,

00:28:02.809 --> 00:28:08.200
the number one star on the Knicks of the time.
I mean I was living it, the peak. And then

00:28:08.200 --> 00:28:15.290
overnight boom, everything's gone. I got a
500k check in my hand and nothing. Nobody

00:28:15.290 --> 00:28:23.770
wants me. And I got so — the rage that I
felt in that moment was Hulk-like rage. I

00:28:23.770 --> 00:28:30.360
was a berserker. The samurai armor, just took
the sword out and I was like, "I am going

00:28:30.360 --> 00:28:36.690
to prove I can do this again. I am going to
show everybody that this was not a fluke."

00:28:36.690 --> 00:28:41.969
It made me — I've never felt more rage in
my life, I don't think. And I've been in some

00:28:41.969 --> 00:28:46.114
pretty big brawls. And so then I just went
and I took the money and then I just went

00:28:46.114 --> 00:28:49.450
back to my, I got an office and the next day
I said, "I've got to build something. I've

00:28:49.450 --> 00:28:50.560
got to build something."
Tim Ferriss: The next day.

00:28:50.560 --> 00:28:55.789
Jason Calacanis: The next day I was like,
literally got an office share with my friend's

00:28:55.789 --> 00:29:00.570
office. I said, "Can I — you got a desk?"
And it was like that blank sheet of paper

00:29:00.570 --> 00:29:06.630
in 2000, 2003. "I've got to figure something
out. What's next?" And I just started making

00:29:06.630 --> 00:29:09.870
designs on whiteboards —
Tim Ferriss: I was going to ask, that day,

00:29:09.870 --> 00:29:12.679
how do you even begin with such a blue sky
—

00:29:12.679 --> 00:29:15.740
Jason Calacanis: Yeah.
Tim Ferriss: Blank page. How did you even

00:29:15.740 --> 00:29:21.360
start? Were you just pulling from random bits
and pieces floating around? Did you have some

00:29:21.360 --> 00:29:23.570
approach?
Jason Calacanis: Approach? I started calling

00:29:23.570 --> 00:29:28.000
people up. I started calling people up, "What
are you working on? What's happening?" And

00:29:28.000 --> 00:29:33.830
then this one person was making this photosharing
site, Flickr. Another person was doing a bookmarking

00:29:33.830 --> 00:29:39.840
site called del.icio.us. There's just this
little underground of people who were building

00:29:39.840 --> 00:29:42.700
stuff for the web because the web had gotten
advanced now in the second decade.

00:29:42.700 --> 00:29:48.899
And it do — the webpages could refresh themselves
with Ajax and there were kind of tables, it

00:29:48.899 --> 00:29:52.980
became more responsive and there were all
these new technical things and editorial formats

00:29:52.980 --> 00:30:00.010
people were playing with. And I was like,
"Huh, who's doing anything?" And then there

00:30:00.010 --> 00:30:06.899
was this one group doing journaling on the
web. There was another group that was trying

00:30:06.899 --> 00:30:14.410
to do photosharing and follow your friends.
And I'm just like, huh. And then I had somebody

00:30:14.410 --> 00:30:16.540
who had worked for me named Rafat Ali and
he had started a blog —

00:30:16.540 --> 00:30:19.549
Tim Ferriss: Let me interrupt for one second.
So the reason you're calling these people

00:30:19.549 --> 00:30:24.750
is not because you want to work with them
on what they're doing necessarily or was it?

00:30:24.750 --> 00:30:27.220
Or were you just trying to get read of the
landscape?

00:30:27.220 --> 00:30:31.930
Jason Calacanis: No, it's literally just trying
to network with people who I had known and

00:30:31.930 --> 00:30:37.560
I was literally asking people, "What's going
on? Is anybody doing anything cool?" And I

00:30:37.560 --> 00:30:41.600
just started looking for people doing cool
projects just to see what was going to happen

00:30:41.600 --> 00:30:45.169
after the dot com bust because everybody quit
and everybody went and got jobs at big companies.

00:30:45.169 --> 00:30:49.750
Tim Ferriss: So you're trying to get a read
of the playing field to see where the pucks

00:30:49.750 --> 00:30:52.070
might be going next?
Jason Calacanis: Exactly. Is there anything

00:30:52.070 --> 00:30:54.429
interesting —
Tim Ferriss: I'm mixing my sports metaphors,

00:30:54.429 --> 00:30:57.100
but you get the idea. Yeah, I'm talking about
field hockey everybody. Yeah. Yeah.

00:30:57.100 --> 00:31:02.840
Jason Calacanis: Let's just say Tim Ferriss,
when he learns a sport, it's wild to watch.

00:31:02.840 --> 00:31:07.350
Watching him first learn a sport is hilarious
because I taught him how to do a layup.

00:31:07.350 --> 00:31:09.630
Tim Ferriss: It is. It is funny.
Jason Calacanis: Taught him how to dribble

00:31:09.630 --> 00:31:12.409
basketball, it was hilarious. That's another
story.

00:31:12.409 --> 00:31:17.029
Tim Ferriss: It is funny. And also if you
learn a sport with J Cal, you are going to

00:31:17.029 --> 00:31:20.330
have to listen to so much shit —
Jason Calacanis: I'm going to torture you.

00:31:20.330 --> 00:31:23.299
Tim Ferriss: He will torture you at every
opportunity. Yeah.

00:31:23.299 --> 00:31:30.200
Jason Calacanis: Watching Tim Ferriss go from
being a perfect fencer or perfect yoga in

00:31:30.200 --> 00:31:34.250
negative a hundred degrees and then he tries
to dribble a basketball, it's literally like

00:31:34.250 --> 00:31:36.910
looking at a drunk person with a blindfold
on.

00:31:36.910 --> 00:31:38.670
Tim Ferriss: Oh yeah.
Jason Calacanis: But to your credit —

00:31:38.670 --> 00:31:40.429
Tim Ferriss: True story.
Jason Calacanis: I watched you learn how to

00:31:40.429 --> 00:31:44.950
do a layup and I gave — you asked me like
three or four questions and you went from

00:31:44.950 --> 00:31:49.940
looking like you were blind and drunk to all
of a sudden doing a layup in under three minutes

00:31:49.940 --> 00:31:54.030
perfectly. And I was like, yeah, that's Tim
Ferriss' gift. He knows how to ask the right

00:31:54.030 --> 00:31:58.019
questions and I'm watching you process. It
was actually impressive.

00:31:58.019 --> 00:32:03.720
But anyway, it was one of those moments where
you're like, "What's next in the world?" I

00:32:03.720 --> 00:32:06.799
was just trying to figure out what was next.
So I was just — because also so many people

00:32:06.799 --> 00:32:12.860
were out of work that if you couldn't get
a job, you kind of just started tinkering.

00:32:12.860 --> 00:32:16.941
Which is kind of how multimedia started. So
it was like, I've seen this before. Technology

00:32:16.941 --> 00:32:20.990
keeps going, people are starting to tinker.
And Wi-Fi had come out at that time, I was

00:32:20.990 --> 00:32:25.730
very fascinated about Wi-Fi and I was thinking
about Wi-Fi routers and putting Wi-Fi in places

00:32:25.730 --> 00:32:28.810
and maybe you could have internet in cafes
and stuff.

00:32:28.810 --> 00:32:32.249
It was just all kinds of crazy little ideas.
And I started whiteboarding stuff. I started

00:32:32.249 --> 00:32:37.679
— Kozmo had gone out of business, the delivery
service. So the first idea I had was for Mercury

00:32:37.679 --> 00:32:43.210
Club. And I was going to get a hundred New
Yorkers and give them Mercury Club number

00:32:43.210 --> 00:32:45.590
one, two, three, four, five, all the way to
a hundred. I was going to give one to Charlie

00:32:45.590 --> 00:32:49.139
Rose, one to Howard Stern. And my idea was
to get a bunch of people on Vespas, because

00:32:49.139 --> 00:32:52.269
I had gotten a Vespa and I was driving around
New York and I realized, because I had driven

00:32:52.269 --> 00:32:55.590
one in Barcelona, I was like, this is the
way to — if you're in a city, a bike's too

00:32:55.590 --> 00:33:00.760
slow, a car's too slow, and a Vespa goes fast
because you can just weave in and out of traffic.

00:33:00.760 --> 00:33:03.809
And I had seen Kozmo had started experimenting
with Vepsas and I said, "I'm going to create

00:33:03.809 --> 00:33:09.870
a service where you pay a hundred dollars
a month for rich people and you get four hours

00:33:09.870 --> 00:33:14.510
of deliveries, 25 bucks an hour."
This is when minimum wage was six or seven

00:33:14.510 --> 00:33:18.610
bucks. But this is only for rich people. And
if you needed a bottle of wine or pack of

00:33:18.610 --> 00:33:24.360
cigarettes, whatever, you would just call
an 800 number. Because 800 numbers were another

00:33:24.360 --> 00:33:29.679
platform, they became zines for a while. 1-800-FLOWERS,
1-800-MATTRESS, 1-800 everything. And so I

00:33:29.679 --> 00:33:33.340
started looking into buying a 1-800 number
because I was just always very interested

00:33:33.340 --> 00:33:36.780
in mediums, right? Because if you get to a
medium first, you can exploit it. Whether

00:33:36.780 --> 00:33:40.840
it's podcasting like you and I did, or blogging
like I did and you did, I think, or zines.

00:33:40.840 --> 00:33:45.429
Tim Ferriss: Yeah. Started in 2005, blog.
Jason Calacanis: Yeah. So if you get to it

00:33:45.429 --> 00:33:49.360
first, you get to play with it first. You
get a disproportionate amount of credit for

00:33:49.360 --> 00:33:55.690
being there first. Just if you go to Austin
first or you go to, Bed-Stuy Brooklyn, first

00:33:55.690 --> 00:33:58.809
you get the cool loft that you bought for
nothing and then everybody else comes and

00:33:58.809 --> 00:34:03.260
your loft goes up in value. So the value of
the platform goes up as the people who tinker

00:34:03.260 --> 00:34:07.429
and create it get to play with it. So I never
really actually thought about it, but it is

00:34:07.429 --> 00:34:13.050
true. And so I also got the 800 number. 1-800-MERCURY,
I was trying to get a Mercury Club number.

00:34:13.050 --> 00:34:16.419
And my idea was the — because remember I
was talking to the guy who had done Kozmo,

00:34:16.419 --> 00:34:20.261
because I covered it in my magazine. And he
said, "Yeah, I should have just charged a

00:34:20.261 --> 00:34:23.500
delivery fee."
Because the last six months they started charging

00:34:23.500 --> 00:34:27.149
a delivery fee. And he said, "Yeah, the business
worked in three of the nine cities. If I had

00:34:27.149 --> 00:34:31.139
only charged delivery fees," I was like, "Why
didn't you?" He's like, "The VCs told me not

00:34:31.139 --> 00:34:34.609
to, wanted to grow. I'm trying to get public."
It was just literally the same lesson people

00:34:34.609 --> 00:34:38.470
are learning now. So I was like, well wait,
instead of charging a delivery fee, why don't

00:34:38.470 --> 00:34:42.950
I just make it — because I knew bike messengers.
Bike messenger culture was a big thing in

00:34:42.950 --> 00:34:46.500
New York. So bike messengers used to hang
out outside where my loft was because it was

00:34:46.500 --> 00:34:50.389
business commercial loft, the Starrett-Lehigh
Building in New York. It was commercial and

00:34:50.389 --> 00:34:54.020
we were living there illegally. Me, DJ Spooky,
a couple of artists were living in these illegal

00:34:54.020 --> 00:34:57.700
lofts. Anyway, the bike messengers were out
there all the time.

00:34:57.700 --> 00:35:02.860
They were always smoking weed. And it was
a very cool culture because they had figured

00:35:02.860 --> 00:35:05.369
out life. They made nine or 10 bucks an hour,
they would make 10 bucks per run. They could

00:35:05.369 --> 00:35:09.670
do two runs an hour. And they got to just
bomb these bikes all over Manhattan. And they

00:35:09.670 --> 00:35:14.200
got exercise, they were fit, they were smoking
weed, listening to music. It was like the

00:35:14.200 --> 00:35:18.510
coolest gig ever. I was like, these guys,
they figured out life. You get to ride your

00:35:18.510 --> 00:35:23.200
bike all day, smoke dope, listen to cool music,
joke with your friends, and you just drop

00:35:23.200 --> 00:35:26.070
off an envelope now and again. And you make
more money than the people you're dropping

00:35:26.070 --> 00:35:30.710
off the envelope to, which are outside. It
was just a crazy revelation I had. So I was

00:35:30.710 --> 00:35:34.240
like, yeah, I'm going to do Mercury Club.
And I built the whole business model for Mercury

00:35:34.240 --> 00:35:36.780
Club and then I was watching the blog —
Tim Ferriss: How did you land on the name?

00:35:36.780 --> 00:35:41.619
Jason Calacanis: Well, Mercury the god with
the wings on the feet and I just came to,

00:35:41.619 --> 00:35:45.120
I was Greek. I always liked the Greek gods.
So I was just like kind of started thinking

00:35:45.120 --> 00:35:48.060
about fast and I just thought, well this would
be incredible. Can you imagine you're like

00:35:48.060 --> 00:35:53.730
Charlie Rose at a party or David Hershkovits
from Paper and you're just like, "Oh, should

00:35:53.730 --> 00:35:56.130
we get cigarettes?" "Yeah." And you just pick
out your — because mobile phones had come

00:35:56.130 --> 00:35:59.640
out at that time. So imagine taking out your
StarTAC, and I had seen a StarTAC phone, those

00:35:59.640 --> 00:36:02.500
little flip phones. It's like imagine you
take that out and you dial an 800 number and

00:36:02.500 --> 00:36:07.800
you say, "Yeah, I need two packs of Marlboro
Lights and bring me a copy of Vanity Fair

00:36:07.800 --> 00:36:11.260
or Esquire magazine, because I'm at a party,
I want to show somebody," and then just somebody

00:36:11.260 --> 00:36:15.640
comes there and hands it to you? The ultimate
luxury, the ultimate power move. I can just

00:36:15.640 --> 00:36:20.320
call my assistant and just get anything I
want. Anyway, I didn't wind up building that.

00:36:20.320 --> 00:36:25.300
Tim Ferriss: So, Greek. Your name is Greek.
What does your last name mean?

00:36:25.300 --> 00:36:31.849
Jason Calacanis: Yeah, so Calacanis means
"to have done well" or "good for you." So

00:36:31.849 --> 00:36:34.619
sometimes when I use my credit card and a
Greek person does it, they're like, "Good

00:36:34.619 --> 00:36:38.109
for you," or "You've done well, very nice."
But it's with Ks. So when my grandfather went

00:36:38.109 --> 00:36:43.570
through Ellis Island, God rest his soul, they
were like, "Calacanis, here's your banana,

00:36:43.570 --> 00:36:47.650
keep moving." And he was like, "No, it's Ks."
And they were just like, "Fuck you, Calacanis."

00:36:47.650 --> 00:36:50.830
Tim Ferriss: "Here's your banana."
Jason Calacanis: No, they used to give bananas

00:36:50.830 --> 00:36:52.550
at Ellis Island, I understand.
Tim Ferriss: What? That's real?

00:36:52.550 --> 00:36:55.290
Jason Calacanis: Yeah. It was some weird thing
where they just would give everybody who comes

00:36:55.290 --> 00:36:58.820
in a banana, because they were hungry off
the boats and bananas were, a lot of bananas

00:36:58.820 --> 00:37:02.069
were coming in from South America to Ellis
Island as well. So they'd just be like, "Have

00:37:02.069 --> 00:37:03.460
a banana."
Tim Ferriss: Today I learned.

00:37:03.460 --> 00:37:07.730
Jason Calacanis: Keep it moving. And then
the crazy thing, Tim, is I was on the Ellis

00:37:07.730 --> 00:37:13.540
Island website and on the Ellis Island website
I searched for my last name and they had archived

00:37:13.540 --> 00:37:17.640
everything. And Calacanis, my great grandfather,
or my grandfather rather, went through 12

00:37:17.640 --> 00:37:20.960
times. And it turned out he was in the Merchant
Marines. He was an inching captain. He wasn't

00:37:20.960 --> 00:37:28.480
— and I knew that, but every time he came
back to America with human passengers, he

00:37:28.480 --> 00:37:32.920
had to sign in again. And so he has gone through
it 12 times. It was quite a weird —

00:37:32.920 --> 00:37:35.520
Tim Ferriss: That's wild.
Jason Calacanis: And I was like, wow, he's

00:37:35.520 --> 00:37:38.870
coming from Brazil, he's coming from Ireland,
he's coming from Italy, he's coming from everywhere

00:37:38.870 --> 00:37:41.214
in the world. The cargo was humans, it was
Americans.

00:37:41.214 --> 00:37:44.390
Tim Ferriss: Human cargo.
Jason Calacanis: It was human cargo. It was

00:37:44.390 --> 00:37:46.140
pretty crazy, right?
Tim Ferriss: That's wild.

00:37:46.140 --> 00:37:50.560
Jason Calacanis: Anyway, then I watched the
blog stuff happen and two people who had worked

00:37:50.560 --> 00:37:55.650
for me, Xeni Jardin was my event producer.
She had been working for a famous law firm,

00:37:55.650 --> 00:37:58.500
Latham Watkins, and then she came — they
were a sponsor of one of my events. She did

00:37:58.500 --> 00:38:02.190
a great job at the event. I asked Latham,
"Can I hire her, because she's awesome and

00:38:02.190 --> 00:38:05.720
rad?" And I hired Xeni to come work with me
at Silicon Alley Reporter, she would produce

00:38:05.720 --> 00:38:12.520
my events. And then when everything shut down,
she started working on this Boing Boing thing.

00:38:12.520 --> 00:38:18.630
And I'm like, "Boing Boing? What are you doing
with your life?" She was like, "Yeah, we write

00:38:18.630 --> 00:38:23.760
blog posts." I'm like, "Blogs? Blogs?" And
I was like, "Like Rafat Ali was doing?" This

00:38:23.760 --> 00:38:29.710
kid who had worked for me, Rafat Ali was doing
a blog on the site called paidContent.org

00:38:29.710 --> 00:38:34.960
and somebody ratted him out when he was working
at the magazine that he was moonlighting.

00:38:34.960 --> 00:38:37.700
Now, back in the day, you couldn't have a
side hustle, that was instant firing.

00:38:37.700 --> 00:38:42.770
So I call him into my office, I'm like, "You
got something to tell me kid?" He's like,

00:38:42.770 --> 00:38:48.020
"No." And I'm like, "You sure you don't have
anything to tell me?" He's like, "Is it about

00:38:48.020 --> 00:38:52.510
paidContent?" And I'm like, "Yeah." I'm like,
"What are you doing?" He's like, "I'm just

00:38:52.510 --> 00:38:56.069
writing about people who are charging for
content on the internet." I'm like, "Hey kid,

00:38:56.069 --> 00:38:59.530
information wants to be free. Nobody's paying
for content on the internet." He goes, "Actually,

00:38:59.530 --> 00:39:05.930
I think you're wrong." "Hold on, kid, let
me stop you. Number one, nobody's paying for

00:39:05.930 --> 00:39:10.619
content. The whole reason we started the internet
is information wants to be free. Number two,

00:39:10.619 --> 00:39:18.369
you're not a great writer. I'm your editor.
Who's your editor for paidContent.org? And

00:39:18.369 --> 00:39:25.730
he goes, "I don't have an editor. It's a blog."
I'm like, "No publication will ever work if

00:39:25.730 --> 00:39:32.640
there is not an editorial structure. It will
fail because there'll be spelling errors.

00:39:32.640 --> 00:39:38.670
You're going to write stupid headlines. You
need an editor. That's how this works."

00:39:38.670 --> 00:39:44.400
He's like, "Am I fired?" I'm like, "No, just
focus on work and stop doing stupid shit on

00:39:44.400 --> 00:39:48.640
the weekend. Get back to your desk." And I
just like admonished Rafat. Now I'm an investor

00:39:48.640 --> 00:39:53.580
in this company now, Skift. But that incepted
in me because when we shut down, I had talked

00:39:53.580 --> 00:39:57.900
to Xeni and I talked to Rafat and Rafat was
like, '"Yeah, I'm making four grand a month

00:39:57.900 --> 00:40:02.810
on ads." I'm like, "I was paying you 35k,
you're making 4k now? You're making 48,000?

00:40:02.810 --> 00:40:07.750
You're making $13,000 more than when you worked
for me." And then Xeni's like, "I'm getting

00:40:07.750 --> 00:40:12.670
a 5k draw a month." I'm like, "What?" I'm
like, "How many hours a day do you work?"

00:40:12.670 --> 00:40:15.560
She's like, "Look, I do like two hours in
the morning, and then at night I come home,

00:40:15.560 --> 00:40:19.200
I do two or three hours." She's like — this
is before The 4-Hour Workweek revolution.

00:40:19.200 --> 00:40:25.660
I mean, this is during the 60-hour reality
of being a worker. And I was like, blogs,

00:40:25.660 --> 00:40:30.960
huh? And then I started reading blogs and
I was like, there's something here. Blogs

00:40:30.960 --> 00:40:36.050
are dope.
Blogs, even though they have spelling errors

00:40:36.050 --> 00:40:41.620
in them, come out faster than magazines and
newspaper articles. They're faster and faster

00:40:41.620 --> 00:40:48.650
is always better. It's faster. I said, and
for the right writer, it's spicy. Like "Ooh,

00:40:48.650 --> 00:40:54.750
Xeni's spicy." And I remember I wouldn't have
let her write that. I would've stopped her.

00:40:54.750 --> 00:40:59.020
And then I checked, just when I saw all the
magazines and everybody had them, I just all

00:40:59.020 --> 00:41:04.170
of a sudden, The Terminator, everything clicked
at once, Tim. I was like, it's faster and

00:41:04.170 --> 00:41:10.490
it's better. It's faster, it's better. It's
cheaper. Faster, better, cheaper. Wait, I

00:41:10.490 --> 00:41:15.560
had heard Marvin Minsky and Kevin Kelly and
all these guys at a conference, a TED at some

00:41:15.560 --> 00:41:19.380
point talking about faster, better, cheaper.
I didn't understand what they meant, but literally

00:41:19.380 --> 00:41:22.900
was at TED and they were at one of Brockman's
dinners and they were talking about "Faster,

00:41:22.900 --> 00:41:25.589
better, cheaper, whatever, pick two."
And I didn't understand what they were talking

00:41:25.589 --> 00:41:31.050
about, but it kind of another Inception moment.
I was like, wait a second, what if you had

00:41:31.050 --> 00:41:35.710
a hundred of them? So then I called Brian
Alvey, who had worked with me on the magazines

00:41:35.710 --> 00:41:41.069
and I said, "Let's go to the next game." And
went to the next game. And I said, "What if

00:41:41.069 --> 00:41:45.430
we had weblogs?" The blogs were originally
called weblogs, because they were on the web

00:41:45.430 --> 00:41:48.040
and they were logs. And then people dropped
the W E and they became blogs. You remember

00:41:48.040 --> 00:41:50.829
all this? But a little history lesson for
those listening.

00:41:50.829 --> 00:41:55.800
And so I was like, what if we did weblogs
for business? Nobody's ever done a business

00:41:55.800 --> 00:42:02.710
topic, so we'll do weblogs. And he goes, "Oh
yeah, we should call it Weblogs, Inc." And

00:42:02.710 --> 00:42:07.430
then he got the domain name and then we started
building them and we did a weblog on Wi-Fi

00:42:07.430 --> 00:42:15.940
and we did a weblog on Apple. And then we
started building all these ones. And in under

00:42:15.940 --> 00:42:22.420
18 months we had built 87 blogs, we had $200,000
in total revenue and AOL bought it for $30

00:42:22.420 --> 00:42:27.910
million. And we had one investor, my friend
Mark Cuban had put $300,000 in for 20 percent

00:42:27.910 --> 00:42:31.770
of the company or something. And I gave him
six million back. And then Brian and I and

00:42:31.770 --> 00:42:36.369
Peter Rojas split the rest. And in 18 months
after my 20 million offer had gone away, I

00:42:36.369 --> 00:42:41.520
got the 30 million offer.
Tim Ferriss: That's just insane. That's just

00:42:41.520 --> 00:42:43.569
insane.
Jason Calacanis: And the berserker insane

00:42:43.569 --> 00:42:49.640
J Cal, "I'm going to prove it to the world,"
proved it. And then I was like, yeah, I just

00:42:49.640 --> 00:42:54.380
looked at the sword. I took the armor off,
hung it up, and I was like, "Yep, I can do

00:42:54.380 --> 00:42:59.400
it. I can do it anytime I want. Anytime I
want, I can build a brand, a business and

00:42:59.400 --> 00:43:03.151
I can just get it from zero to whatever."
I may fail two out of three times, but I could

00:43:03.151 --> 00:43:07.089
do it. And that was when it was like, okay,
once you're lucky, once you're lucky twice

00:43:07.089 --> 00:43:08.220
—
Tim Ferriss: Once you're lucky, twice you're

00:43:08.220 --> 00:43:09.750
good. Yeah.
Jason Calacanis: It was my once you're lucky,

00:43:09.750 --> 00:43:13.330
twice you're good moment. Because then everybody
who had watched me fail and kind of danced

00:43:13.330 --> 00:43:17.390
on my grave at Silicon Alley Reporter was
like, "Oh God. J Cal did it again."

00:43:17.390 --> 00:43:21.600
Tim Ferriss: The cat came back the very next
day. We thought he was gone, but he wouldn't

00:43:21.600 --> 00:43:23.821
stay away.
Jason Calacanis: There's a lot of people who

00:43:23.821 --> 00:43:27.950
are just like, "Oh, he's going to be insufferable
after this." They weren't wrong.

00:43:27.950 --> 00:43:34.910
Tim Ferriss: They weren't wrong. They weren't
wrong. So just a couple of maybe boring housekeeping

00:43:34.910 --> 00:43:41.579
questions. I'm thinking of the timing of building
these weblogs. How did you find the writers

00:43:41.579 --> 00:43:44.170
to do this?
Jason Calacanis: A great thing for people

00:43:44.170 --> 00:43:50.560
to understand right now, in this moment of
time, we're taping this in 2022 with the market

00:43:50.560 --> 00:43:53.950
crash and Facebook laid off 11,000 people
yesterday. Yeah. I don't want to date this

00:43:53.950 --> 00:43:55.359
too much. I don't know when you're going to
release it.

00:43:55.359 --> 00:43:59.059
Tim Ferriss: Oh, that's okay. It'll come out
reasonably soon. But as a snapshot in time,

00:43:59.059 --> 00:44:04.891
also crypto is having a complete implosion.
FTX is oing through the meat grinder. People

00:44:04.891 --> 00:44:08.190
are probably going to go to jail.
Jason Calacanis: Yeah. Jail, 32 billion eviscerated.

00:44:08.190 --> 00:44:12.329
I actually wanted to talk to you about people
who commit fraud and lie because I was interested

00:44:12.329 --> 00:44:13.329
in your perspective on it.
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.

00:44:13.329 --> 00:44:17.520
Jason Calacanis: I just can't imagine it because
the system is so easy to win and the system

00:44:17.520 --> 00:44:20.770
is set up for entrepreneurs to win in this
country. Why on Earth would you cheat if it's

00:44:20.770 --> 00:44:24.490
so easy to just have a win by just working
hard? Anyway, putting that aside.

00:44:24.490 --> 00:44:28.280
Tim Ferriss: But I interrupted you because
you were tying the weblogs and —

00:44:28.280 --> 00:44:32.650
Jason Calacanis: Oh, the writers. Yeah. So
it was very simple. Everybody was out of work.

00:44:32.650 --> 00:44:37.270
And so when you hired a writer, you'd say,
"Do you know anybody?" And the original deal

00:44:37.270 --> 00:44:42.030
was we started going to people who were doing
LiveJournal blogs and we'd find somebody who

00:44:42.030 --> 00:44:47.430
had written a blog about Apple, Sean Bonner.
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, Sean Bonner! Yeah, yeah.

00:44:47.430 --> 00:44:50.160
Jason Calacanis: And I said, "Hey, Sean, I'm
doing this Weblogs, Inc. thing, we're going

00:44:50.160 --> 00:44:54.010
to try to sell ads on blogs." He's like, "Well,
that's stupid." I'm like, "Yeah, but I think

00:44:54.010 --> 00:44:58.250
I could figure it out because I did a magazine."
He's like, "All right, that's pretty smart."

00:44:58.250 --> 00:45:01.660
I was like, "I see you're writing some Apple
blogs. Could we put them on our Apple blog?

00:45:01.660 --> 00:45:05.400
Or if I gave you the login, would you post
them?" Because Brian Alvey was a genius. He's

00:45:05.400 --> 00:45:08.420
now running WordPress for Matt Mullenweg.
Tim Ferriss: Oh, no kidding.

00:45:08.420 --> 00:45:10.790
Jason Calacanis: WordPress, like Pro, VIP
like people like you and I use.

00:45:10.790 --> 00:45:13.870
Tim Ferriss: At Automattic. Yeah.
Jason Calacanis: At Automattic. So anyway,

00:45:13.870 --> 00:45:19.560
he was a great collaborator of my career.
He's kind of my George Harrison or he's my

00:45:19.560 --> 00:45:23.839
Paul to my Lennon or vice versa. And he had
built the software that was better than everybody's,

00:45:23.839 --> 00:45:27.700
better than Typeform, better than Blogger,
better than everything. And I just gave Sean

00:45:27.700 --> 00:45:32.280
the login and I was like, "Listen, I want
to pay you something because I want to own

00:45:32.280 --> 00:45:37.730
the blog posts." He's like, "You want to own
a paragraph of me writing about a MacBook?

00:45:37.730 --> 00:45:43.780
It's three sentences, Jason, it's worthless.
I'll do it for free." And I was like, "I'll

00:45:43.780 --> 00:45:47.930
tell you what. How many a day could you do
about Apple products?" He's like, "Two." I

00:45:47.930 --> 00:45:55.790
was like, "Okay, two, 60, I'll give you 150
bucks a month, cash, to just let us run these

00:45:55.790 --> 00:45:58.849
over here. It's a paragraph each."
He's like, "I can write these in 10 minutes.

00:45:58.849 --> 00:46:04.329
You're an idiot, but sure, I'll take whatever
it is, two or $3 a blog post." And so we just

00:46:04.329 --> 00:46:07.690
then just started hiring people, and then
we made five bucks a blog post, eventually

00:46:07.690 --> 00:46:11.130
10 bucks a blog post. And people thought we
were stupid because minimum wage at the time

00:46:11.130 --> 00:46:14.420
was 10 bucks. And people were like, "I wrote
three blog posts this hour and you gave me

00:46:14.420 --> 00:46:20.020
$30." But I had done a spreadsheet with Brian
and I said, "Here's how many page views we're

00:46:20.020 --> 00:46:24.849
getting. Here's what I think we can make per
page view. Here's what the cost is per blog

00:46:24.849 --> 00:46:30.079
post. All we have to do with this business
is scale it and then somebody will buy it."

00:46:30.079 --> 00:46:34.020
And he said, "Yeah, I think you're right."
I said, "You do the tech, I'll get the writers

00:46:34.020 --> 00:46:38.730
and the advertising. I'll see you on the other
side." And we both put our heads down for

00:46:38.730 --> 00:46:43.690
18 months, and we had 450 bloggers in the
system, of which maybe 200 were active. And

00:46:43.690 --> 00:46:48.760
we were pounding out thousands of blog posts
a week across 87 different topics. And then

00:46:48.760 --> 00:46:52.089
AOL found out about what we're doing. And
they had looked at Gawker and they had looked

00:46:52.089 --> 00:46:56.080
at us and Engadget was crushing Gawker because
we stole Peter Rojas from Gizmodo. It's a

00:46:56.080 --> 00:46:58.680
whole other story.
Nick Denton and I had quite a rivalry for

00:46:58.680 --> 00:47:03.829
a while. It was a fun rivalry too. But Peter
Rojas was actually the missing piece with

00:47:03.829 --> 00:47:08.140
Brian Alvey and I, and once we got Peter Rojas,
he taught us like, "Hey idiots, just come

00:47:08.140 --> 00:47:12.859
up with a name for the blogs. I don't want
to be gadget.weblogsinc." So then we made

00:47:12.859 --> 00:47:16.930
the apple.weblogsinc, The Unofficial Apple
Weblog, TUAW, T-U-A-W, which people fell in

00:47:16.930 --> 00:47:21.050
love with. People were addicted to TUAW because
at that point we were doing eight or nine

00:47:21.050 --> 00:47:26.660
blog post a day about Apple. And Steve Jobs
read it. And Jony Ive and everybody and then

00:47:26.660 --> 00:47:31.830
Engadget became a juggernaut. And it was just
a very simple business. And at that time,

00:47:31.830 --> 00:47:35.210
$30 million was the equivalent of hundreds
of millions now.

00:47:35.210 --> 00:47:37.060
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, for sure.
Jason Calacanis: It was a different era.

00:47:37.060 --> 00:47:40.500
Tim Ferriss: It's still, I mean, 30 million
today would still a lot good chunk of change.

00:47:40.500 --> 00:47:46.150
So let me flash forward and I want to ask
you specifically, because it came up already

00:47:46.150 --> 00:47:52.900
about events. So you seem to love producing
events and you've produced a lot of events.

00:47:52.900 --> 00:47:56.470
I only produced one event —
Jason Calacanis: In Reno.

00:47:56.470 --> 00:47:58.020
Tim Ferriss: It was very, very —
Jason Calacanis: Oh, Tahoe.

00:47:58.020 --> 00:48:00.290
Tim Ferriss: It was in Napa long ago. And
it was so —

00:48:00.290 --> 00:48:02.050
Jason Calacanis: I have a friend who went,
she loved it.

00:48:02.050 --> 00:48:03.890
Tim Ferriss: It was a great event. It was
—

00:48:03.890 --> 00:48:06.579
Jason Calacanis: People still talk about this
event. Somebody brought it up last year.

00:48:06.579 --> 00:48:09.390
Tim Ferriss: They do. They do. And it was
opening, it was called Opening the Kimono,

00:48:09.390 --> 00:48:15.420
OTK. I actually have a wine bottle from that
event over there. Because we had a wine making

00:48:15.420 --> 00:48:20.849
competition that the participants all engaged
in. And then we took the winner and we bottled

00:48:20.849 --> 00:48:25.990
whatever it was, 200 bottles and gave them
to everybody. So it was incredibly elaborate.

00:48:25.990 --> 00:48:32.460
And I came out of that feeling like I had
just finished a tour of duty. It was so hard.

00:48:32.460 --> 00:48:38.280
Why do you love doing events and what is the
smart way to do it? What is the J Cal recipe?

00:48:38.280 --> 00:48:41.970
Jason Calacanis: So in the Myers-Briggs, I'm
going to say you're INTJ or INTP.

00:48:41.970 --> 00:48:45.800
Tim Ferriss: Nailed it, INTJ.
Jason Calacanis: Yeah. But I could see you

00:48:45.800 --> 00:48:51.790
being a perceiver. But anyway, I'm ENTJ. And
so you and I vibe until such time as I absolutely

00:48:51.790 --> 00:48:57.200
exhaust you, and you need to go to your room
and take a deep breath. That's the relationship

00:48:57.200 --> 00:49:02.210
between INTJs and ENTJs. This is for people
who don't know the Myers-Briggs, which is,

00:49:02.210 --> 00:49:07.490
or as we call it, astrology for men.
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, that and Enneagrams. Astrology

00:49:07.490 --> 00:49:11.020
for men. That's basically it.
Jason Calacanis: We don't believe in astrology,

00:49:11.020 --> 00:49:15.161
but we believe in this other thing where we
answer questions and it tells us things we

00:49:15.161 --> 00:49:19.660
want to hear about ourselves. But anyway,
I scored 86 and a hundred percent on entrepreneurship.

00:49:19.660 --> 00:49:23.359
I'm sorry, on extroversion, the two times
I took it. So I'm so extroverted that when

00:49:23.359 --> 00:49:27.890
I'm with an introvert like yourself, or say,
Evan Williams, a friend of ours, I can burn

00:49:27.890 --> 00:49:33.490
people out. I want to talk for seven hours.
And when I've been on vacation with Tim Ferriss,

00:49:33.490 --> 00:49:36.760
it's like Tim Ferriss was like, "Well, that
was an interesting 90 minutes with J Cal,

00:49:36.760 --> 00:49:41.030
I need to go write for four hours," or whatever.
You need to recharge your batteries.

00:49:41.030 --> 00:49:43.340
Tim Ferriss: Take a nap.
Jason Calacanis: Take a nap, whatever. And

00:49:43.340 --> 00:49:47.340
if I talk for seven hours, I want to talk
for five more. I just can't go to bed or I

00:49:47.340 --> 00:49:51.230
want to start working. And so I think you
have to understand your format, I think the

00:49:51.230 --> 00:49:57.710
reason why you are the number one guy in podcasting
is because this format works really well for

00:49:57.710 --> 00:50:03.810
you. You take a lot of time to think about
it. You do your 90 minutes and then you got

00:50:03.810 --> 00:50:08.869
a lot of time to decompress and process. For
me, I do my show five days a week and All-In

00:50:08.869 --> 00:50:12.730
six days a week. I like to talk every day.
So once you understand I'm like the morning

00:50:12.730 --> 00:50:20.190
radio guy, right? And you're like the weekly
60 Minutes guy, right? And viva la difference,

00:50:20.190 --> 00:50:26.510
the problem with events is some people who
are introvert, if they do an event, they need

00:50:26.510 --> 00:50:31.109
recovery time.
And so for you to do an event, it should be

00:50:31.109 --> 00:50:36.119
you interview somebody on stage, you then
go backstage and write some more notes, and

00:50:36.119 --> 00:50:39.510
then somebody else interviews somebody and
then you come back out and do a Q&amp;A with the

00:50:39.510 --> 00:50:43.369
audience and then the events over and that's
the totality of the events. I did a bunch

00:50:43.369 --> 00:50:48.250
of events because I realized if you could
manifest a community in a real world space,

00:50:48.250 --> 00:50:54.730
again, back to my childhood, need to be important
or have power, which was based on having no

00:50:54.730 --> 00:51:01.099
power and no money. And I feel like I've kind
of processed that now. I kind of processed

00:51:01.099 --> 00:51:07.920
it, if I'm honest, maybe 15, 20 years ago,
I kind of figured it out. But I love the idea

00:51:07.920 --> 00:51:13.700
that if you host the party, those people get
to meet new people, create some relationship

00:51:13.700 --> 00:51:17.530
or moment that then changes their life forever.
And so what you should be very proud of with

00:51:17.530 --> 00:51:22.630
your Open the Kimono event is that to this
day, people talk about it as a peak experience

00:51:22.630 --> 00:51:28.099
in their life. And when you think about what
our lives are, in some ways it's just like

00:51:28.099 --> 00:51:34.180
Blade Runner when the Nexus-6 is on the roof
and he says like, "Oh, my God, all my memories

00:51:34.180 --> 00:51:37.950
are going to be gone like tears in the rain."
All we have at the end of this, I believe,

00:51:37.950 --> 00:51:43.710
I'm not a religious guy, I'm an atheist. But
I do know that that singular moment in film

00:51:43.710 --> 00:51:48.650
is the most powerful moment in any film I've
ever seen and is kind of my spirit moment

00:51:48.650 --> 00:51:56.530
of any piece of cinema or perhaps even literature,
that speech he gives, because that's what

00:51:56.530 --> 00:52:01.710
we, I think human existence is about at its
core. It's a series of memories you collect

00:52:01.710 --> 00:52:05.970
over time.
You and I playing basketball on that court,

00:52:05.970 --> 00:52:11.141
in the woods, in Italy, laughing our asses
off and then you figuring it out, right? It's

00:52:11.141 --> 00:52:16.310
just like this weird moment in time that when
I just start describing it to you, you and

00:52:16.310 --> 00:52:20.690
I just start laughing where it's just a great
Tim and J Cal moment that nobody else was

00:52:20.690 --> 00:52:24.960
there for, is just the two of us literally
in a basketball court hidden in the woods

00:52:24.960 --> 00:52:29.550
in on a farm in Italy somewhere that we discovered.
It's a really beautiful memory for me and

00:52:29.550 --> 00:52:30.890
I think for you.
Tim Ferriss: And for me too.

00:52:30.890 --> 00:52:33.700
Jason Calacanis: And you just, then we'll
get to the end of our lives and just that

00:52:33.700 --> 00:52:39.390
Nexus-6 that has this four-year lifespan,
and he's crying for the lost memories. That's

00:52:39.390 --> 00:52:45.910
all you have. And then gone like tears in
the rain. So make those memories. And that

00:52:45.910 --> 00:52:47.461
Open the Kimono event, I was literally talking
to somebody, not, yeah, I was talking to them

00:52:47.461 --> 00:52:49.340
about it on Halloween. It was just two weeks
ago.

00:52:49.340 --> 00:52:52.210
Tim Ferriss: Very recent.
Jason Calacanis: And she was describing how

00:52:52.210 --> 00:52:55.700
that was her favorite event. I was like, "You've
been to mine?" And she's like, "Yeah, but

00:52:55.700 --> 00:52:59.300
that was the one I liked best." And so you
could be proud of, even if that was the only

00:52:59.300 --> 00:53:02.690
memory that happened there, that was the only
tear in the rain, you'll always have that,

00:53:02.690 --> 00:53:07.870
right? And so for me, that's what it is. People
have these incredible moments and I have them,

00:53:07.870 --> 00:53:13.910
and I have now, I'm 51, I'm just looking at
the next number of years we have left. And

00:53:13.910 --> 00:53:18.370
people of our age, like Dave Goldberg passed,
Tony Hsieh passed, a lot of our friends are

00:53:18.370 --> 00:53:20.060
gone.
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, it's wild.

00:53:20.060 --> 00:53:24.299
Jason Calacanis: Gone too soon. And now I
just look at it, I'm like, "How many more

00:53:24.299 --> 00:53:30.180
memories can I put into the memory bank before
the tears in the rain moment?"

00:53:30.180 --> 00:53:36.410
Tim Ferriss: So let's talk about friendship,
because there are a few things that come to

00:53:36.410 --> 00:53:42.230
mind when I think J Cal and friendship. I
wanted to actually sidestep and just mention

00:53:42.230 --> 00:53:45.090
something that ties back to a few things you
said that I was going to bring up later, but

00:53:45.090 --> 00:53:50.630
I'm just going to bookmark this one. But this
is a quote from October, 2008 when a lot of

00:53:50.630 --> 00:53:54.820
shit was going down. People may or may not
remember, if you weren't aware of it, a lot

00:53:54.820 --> 00:53:59.930
of things were imploding. And your quote at
the time in The Guardian is, "Everybody else

00:53:59.930 --> 00:54:03.280
is going to be depressed and drinking and
not working, so it's a great time to be an

00:54:03.280 --> 00:54:08.270
entrepreneur." So that ties into a lot of
what you already said, but on friends, I want

00:54:08.270 --> 00:54:16.200
to say two things related to that. The first
is, let me find this amazing quote. So this

00:54:16.200 --> 00:54:22.069
is a quote from Douglas Rushkoff.
Jason Calacanis: Oh, this is a good one. I

00:54:22.069 --> 00:54:25.569
put this on the back of my book.
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, yeah. "Jason would never

00:54:25.569 --> 00:54:31.359
stab you in the back. He might stab you in
the face though." So I really admire you as

00:54:31.359 --> 00:54:36.580
a friend. And this is not just for me, this
is related to other people because you are

00:54:36.580 --> 00:54:42.710
one of the most loyal friends I have come
across in my adult life, and I've seen you

00:54:42.710 --> 00:54:51.619
defend your friends in so many circumstances
where, A, you don't have to out of pure self-interest.

00:54:51.619 --> 00:54:57.570
B, there's a very good chance it's never going
to get back to the other person. So I have

00:54:57.570 --> 00:55:01.849
a question for you, and maybe that'll be the
first one about loyalty. The second question,

00:55:01.849 --> 00:55:07.280
because I do want to talk about All-In, is
going to be how you navigated starting a project

00:55:07.280 --> 00:55:12.690
with your very close friends and the conflict
that ensued because I have always avoided

00:55:12.690 --> 00:55:18.619
working, by and large, with my friends. So
loyalty, where does that come from and why?

00:55:18.619 --> 00:55:23.020
And then the navigating, starting a project
with your close friends.

00:55:23.020 --> 00:55:27.930
Jason Calacanis: Well, so much of what makes
us who we are is I think formed in childhood.

00:55:27.930 --> 00:55:34.119
So when you're a kid, I think you have this
computer hard drive operating system and things

00:55:34.119 --> 00:55:38.660
start getting written to it. And one of the
things that my dad wrote into it is like,

00:55:38.660 --> 00:55:41.869
"Hey, you and your brothers, you're The Three
Musketeers. You've got to be there for each

00:55:41.869 --> 00:55:46.510
other. Loyalty above everything else. You
always have to have each other's back." And

00:55:46.510 --> 00:55:51.960
that wasn't just his operating principle.
It was "You're going to get in a fight on

00:55:51.960 --> 00:55:56.570
the way to school. We live in Brooklyn, this
is a dog-eat-dog world. I want to make sure

00:55:56.570 --> 00:56:00.440
that you two, you never leave your brothers
behind." So that was one of his rules. If

00:56:00.440 --> 00:56:03.660
he saw two of us, he wanted to know, "Where's
your brother? Go get your brother."

00:56:03.660 --> 00:56:07.150
Doesn't matter, it's the oldest, the middle,
I'm the middle, or the youngest. You three

00:56:07.150 --> 00:56:11.590
have to be by each other's side all the time.
Never leave one of your brothers behind. Never

00:56:11.590 --> 00:56:15.180
let anybody say anything to your brother.
Always fight. It was one of the great things

00:56:15.180 --> 00:56:22.569
my dad taught me and it stayed with me. And
over time, what I realized was one of my superpowers

00:56:22.569 --> 00:56:27.630
is being there for my friends and being a
loyal friend. But I guess the Doug Rushkoff

00:56:27.630 --> 00:56:33.619
quote, I might be full contact at times where
if I disagree with somebody —

00:56:33.619 --> 00:56:38.140
Tim Ferriss: At times, at times.
Jason Calacanis: At times I might be like,

00:56:38.140 --> 00:56:42.660
"I don't agree with you." Or, "That's stupid."
Tim Ferriss: "Timmy, that's the ugliest layup

00:56:42.660 --> 00:56:44.670
attempt I've ever seen."
Jason Calacanis: "Jesus, Timmy."

00:56:44.670 --> 00:56:46.430
Tim Ferriss: "What the fuck is wrong with
you?"

00:56:46.430 --> 00:56:50.270
Jason Calacanis: "What the fuck is wrong with
you? I showed you there's a little square

00:56:50.270 --> 00:56:55.840
box there. Just aim for the box. You missed
the entire backboard. There's a little box

00:56:55.840 --> 00:57:01.260
behind the rim. Is there's anyway you can
put the ball in the box? Your chances go up

00:57:01.260 --> 00:57:04.490
dramatically if you hit that box." You're
like, "Okay, I got it. I'll put it in the

00:57:04.490 --> 00:57:07.550
box."
But anyway, I like to laugh. I like to be

00:57:07.550 --> 00:57:12.660
loyal. And I think if I have a large number
of acquaintances and then I have a small number

00:57:12.660 --> 00:57:21.069
of very close friends, and that seems to me
to be a good philosophy. And then somewhere

00:57:21.069 --> 00:57:25.429
along the lines, people become transactional
with you, you've had this experience. We've

00:57:25.429 --> 00:57:30.480
talked about it before. Everybody wants something
from Tim Ferriss. Everybody wants Jason to

00:57:30.480 --> 00:57:34.910
fix their problems. Or they think they get
a meeting with somebody who's more famous

00:57:34.910 --> 00:57:39.650
than the two of us put together, that magically
is going to change their life because they

00:57:39.650 --> 00:57:42.420
got to talk to the person and tell them their
ideas.

00:57:42.420 --> 00:57:46.849
I'm like, okay, you're delusional. This person
does not care about your ideas and they're

00:57:46.849 --> 00:57:50.780
busy. So let Travis finish building Uber,
he doesn't need to talk to you about your

00:57:50.780 --> 00:57:56.070
startup. That's not going to make any difference.
And there's something really special about

00:57:56.070 --> 00:58:01.180
being able to be supportive of your friends
and being there for them, because as you become

00:58:01.180 --> 00:58:06.340
more successful, the world, people think the
world gets bigger. Oh, you have more money.

00:58:06.340 --> 00:58:11.280
Oh, your podcast went up the rankings. Oh,
Tim Ferriss is on his 12th best seller. The

00:58:11.280 --> 00:58:12.360
world gets smaller and the number of people
—

00:58:12.360 --> 00:58:15.470
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, it gets a lot smaller.
A lot smaller.

00:58:15.470 --> 00:58:20.299
Jason Calacanis: And then you're sitting home
on a Saturday night when you're famous or

00:58:20.299 --> 00:58:23.970
affluent or rich or whatever combination of
things you succeeded in your life. And it's

00:58:23.970 --> 00:58:30.880
a Saturday night and you're like, going out
to this event is going to be arduous and painful

00:58:30.880 --> 00:58:36.880
rather than delightful. And what I realized
is for people who have this in even a more

00:58:36.880 --> 00:58:44.359
acute fashion than you or me, you have to
create, I think a safe space. A place where

00:58:44.359 --> 00:58:50.180
you can have true friendship and talk about
things that matter. Your kids, your friendships,

00:58:50.180 --> 00:58:54.880
your spouses, your life, your hopes, your
dreams, whatever. Or just shoot the shit and

00:58:54.880 --> 00:58:59.990
watch a movie and laugh. And so it's kind
of one of the great things in life, I think

00:58:59.990 --> 00:59:02.510
is friendships and people. You get what you
put in, you get what — the friendships you

00:59:02.510 --> 00:59:05.339
get are the effort you put into the friendships
you have.

00:59:05.339 --> 00:59:10.160
And so people don't invest in it. And if people
are so transactional, it's actually kind of

00:59:10.160 --> 00:59:15.890
weird. I was at a Halloween party and a lot
of people were coming up to take pictures.

00:59:15.890 --> 00:59:19.770
And I don't mind it at all, I'll take a hundred
pictures in a row. It's fantastic. You listen

00:59:19.770 --> 00:59:23.470
to the pod, I'm super happy. I suppose for
you it's a little bit annoying, with the extrovert/introvert

00:59:23.470 --> 00:59:27.850
thing. I bet you can get through about three
of them before you feel like you need to leave

00:59:27.850 --> 00:59:30.231
the party.
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, then I need a bathroom

00:59:30.231 --> 00:59:33.609
break. I do that at dinners. I mean, I'll
admit something embarrassing, but you won't

00:59:33.609 --> 00:59:38.440
be surprised. Maybe people listening will.
If I go to a dinner with or an event with

00:59:38.440 --> 00:59:45.470
more than eight or ten people, I will leave
the dinner every 10 minutes to go into the

00:59:45.470 --> 00:59:51.220
bathroom and just close my eyes and breathe.
I will disappear. And people are like, "What's

00:59:51.220 --> 00:59:56.960
wrong with his prostate?" But it's actually
fine. I'm just so overwhelmed by the stimulation

00:59:56.960 --> 01:00:01.880
that I need to take breaks. It's really —
Jason Calacanis: Well. And now imagine it's

01:00:01.880 --> 01:00:08.520
people who are a fan of yours or think that
they can use you to achieve some goal, right?

01:00:08.520 --> 01:00:15.020
And literally all these influencers, this
party, really their goal is to just get a

01:00:15.020 --> 01:00:22.010
picture. And I just had this profound sadness
for them. I'm like, if I was meeting me and

01:00:22.010 --> 01:00:27.280
I was an entrepreneur, like a photo's great,
but if you're a first time entrepreneur, maybe

01:00:27.280 --> 01:00:32.740
you should ask me about startups and running
them or raising money or there's a million

01:00:32.740 --> 01:00:40.359
things we could discuss.
But the goal was to get a selfie? Who cares?

01:00:40.359 --> 01:00:44.900
So anyway, it's important to be, I think,
a really good, loyal friend. I have been burned

01:00:44.900 --> 01:00:48.589
in this regard where I've been more loyal
to certain people and they have not been loyal

01:00:48.589 --> 01:00:53.609
to me. And it's very frustrating for some
of the people around me when they see that

01:00:53.609 --> 01:01:00.750
happen to me. And for me, I say that is that
person's chance to learn how to be a friend,

01:01:00.750 --> 01:01:05.580
they just haven't gotten there yet. They don't
understand how much more special it would

01:01:05.580 --> 01:01:11.190
be if they were reciprocating the friendship
I was showing them. And it makes it just even

01:01:11.190 --> 01:01:16.839
more rich. For All-In, it is a very interesting
thing because you and I are solo acts.

01:01:16.839 --> 01:01:21.760
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.
Jason Calacanis: And you have one duet you

01:01:21.760 --> 01:01:23.280
do. It's my favorite part of what you do is
—

01:01:23.280 --> 01:01:24.451
Tim Ferriss: The Random Show!
Jason Calacanis: With Kevin.

01:01:24.451 --> 01:01:26.960
Tim Ferriss: Kevin Rose.
Jason Calacanis: Literally has had profound

01:01:26.960 --> 01:01:28.549
impact on my life.
Tim Ferriss: Oh wow.

01:01:28.549 --> 01:01:31.460
Jason Calacanis: Big part of why I lost 30
pounds this year. You were telling me how

01:01:31.460 --> 01:01:35.880
good I looked, but I was in Italy a couple
of summers ago and you and Kevin were talking

01:01:35.880 --> 01:01:41.810
about Ozempic and you had taken Ozempic and
puked and I went to my doctor and I talked

01:01:41.810 --> 01:01:44.780
to them about Ozempic, like a TV commercial.
And I haven't talked about this previously,

01:01:44.780 --> 01:01:50.119
because I don't want to influence people.
But I did a couple of series of Ozempics along

01:01:50.119 --> 01:01:53.760
with fasting that I learned about from you
and Kevin and using the Zero fasting app,

01:01:53.760 --> 01:01:58.630
which I learned about from the show. And then
I obviously hear you talk about weightlifting

01:01:58.630 --> 01:02:04.089
and the impact that has. And so for the past
year or so, I just did the Tim Ferriss, Kevin

01:02:04.089 --> 01:02:09.110
Rose program. I did a couple cycles of Ozempic.
I did the weightlifting and I did the intermittent

01:02:09.110 --> 01:02:12.210
fasting using the Zero app. And yeah, I dropped
30 pounds and I —

01:02:12.210 --> 01:02:15.600
Tim Ferriss: You look great, man. I said it
before we started recording. I was like, you

01:02:15.600 --> 01:02:18.860
look really, really good. Well done.
Jason Calacanis: Would like to get five or

01:02:18.860 --> 01:02:23.839
10 more pounds of weight, muscle, and be able
to run a marathon again. So we get those two

01:02:23.839 --> 01:02:25.342
goals, I'd be pretty happy.
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, you can do that. You can

01:02:25.342 --> 01:02:27.300
do it You can definitely do it.
Jason Calacanis: I'm in the window.

01:02:27.300 --> 01:02:33.809
Tim Ferriss: Yeah. So solo act, suddenly decides,
wait a minute, I want to be in a rock band,

01:02:33.809 --> 01:02:35.829
but I may —
Jason Calacanis: Did not decide that actually.

01:02:35.829 --> 01:02:39.200
I got tricked into it, if I'm being honest.
Tim Ferriss: So tell the story.

01:02:39.200 --> 01:02:43.160
Jason Calacanis: Well, no, what happened was
Chamath and I had become friends. Chamath

01:02:43.160 --> 01:02:44.599
Palihapitiya.
Tim Ferriss: And you should explain. If people

01:02:44.599 --> 01:02:46.960
have no context, what is All-In? What is the
premise?

01:02:46.960 --> 01:02:50.630
Jason Calacanis: All-In is a podcast with
four friends who are all capital allocators,

01:02:50.630 --> 01:02:54.761
in other words, like, venture capitalist investors
and business builders in — yeah, and I guess

01:02:54.761 --> 01:02:59.270
in all cases. And so four folks who are 50
years old, with 30 years of business experience

01:02:59.270 --> 01:03:04.220
each, so that's about over 120 years building
businesses or investing in them, getting together

01:03:04.220 --> 01:03:08.289
and talking about business, and then whatever
the topics of the day are. But everybody's

01:03:08.289 --> 01:03:13.200
a little unique and different. And it started
as something we did in COVID, because we would

01:03:13.200 --> 01:03:19.029
all play poker together. And four of the poker
players, eventually, wound up doing this every

01:03:19.029 --> 01:03:24.460
week, this podcast, and this is in decade
two of me podcasting. And so I kind of created

01:03:24.460 --> 01:03:29.829
a format that I modeled after The McLaughlin
Group, which was a Sunday morning show.

01:03:29.829 --> 01:03:31.599
Tim Ferriss: Don't know it.
Jason Calacanis: Yeah. So yeah, there's a

01:03:31.599 --> 01:03:34.010
guy, McLaughlin. You can look up some tapes
of it.

01:03:34.010 --> 01:03:39.180
Anyway, this guy McLaughlin was super cantankerous
and he'd have a Republican, a Democrat, an

01:03:39.180 --> 01:03:44.640
Independent, whatever, a young person, a Bostonian
person, a California hippie, whatever. And

01:03:44.640 --> 01:03:48.440
he was always cantankerous and moving the
conversation along and he would say, "Wrong!"

01:03:48.440 --> 01:03:54.710
and do this. And it was a very full contact,
kind of dynamic, interrupt each other conversation.

01:03:54.710 --> 01:04:00.510
But it always drew me in. It was the best
Sunday morning political show of all time,

01:04:00.510 --> 01:04:04.511
The McLaughlin Group.
And so Chamath and I had known each other

01:04:04.511 --> 01:04:09.369
from AOL. He was working at AOL when I sold
my company there, the blogging company, back

01:04:09.369 --> 01:04:13.869
in the day. We had met. He became a venture
capitalist and then he met Mark Zuckerberg

01:04:13.869 --> 01:04:18.090
and he was one of the first employees at Facebook.
Grew Facebook from 10 million to 800 million

01:04:18.090 --> 01:04:22.230
users or something crazy like that. He was
responsible for the growth team. Super intelligent,

01:04:22.230 --> 01:04:26.640
considerate, fun, thoughtful guy, and dynamic,
aggressive, and just great leader of people.

01:04:26.640 --> 01:04:32.710
We became friends, we started playing cards
together, joking, laughing, and I said, "I

01:04:32.710 --> 01:04:35.549
really would like to have you on my podcast."
He wanted to be under the radar. He didn't

01:04:35.549 --> 01:04:38.960
want people to know who Chamath Palihapitiya
was. But he came on the podcast. In a lot

01:04:38.960 --> 01:04:43.560
of ways, like Chris Sacca did, our friend,
I think I was one of the first people to actually

01:04:43.560 --> 01:04:49.539
have him on a podcast when I did my two-parter
with him. So Chamath became a bit of a brand

01:04:49.539 --> 01:04:53.200
after he was on the podcast a couple times.
And then he keynoted a couple of events and

01:04:53.200 --> 01:04:57.119
we just had this rapport. And the rapport
came from just breaking chops and laughing

01:04:57.119 --> 01:05:01.200
at the poker game every week. And then people
saw that rapport when we were on stage. And

01:05:01.200 --> 01:05:06.109
then he said to me one time when he was coming
out of CNBC, "We should just do a podcast

01:05:06.109 --> 01:05:07.630
together."
I was like, "Yeah, come on This Week in Startups."

01:05:07.630 --> 01:05:11.910
He said, "No, no, I want to do a different
type of thing, just me and you. You just bring

01:05:11.910 --> 01:05:15.220
up the news of the week, I talk about it,
then I ask you a question, and then you talk

01:05:15.220 --> 01:05:19.589
about it, and we go back and forth. Startups,
public markets, specs, public private." I

01:05:19.589 --> 01:05:22.440
was like, "That's a really good idea. Sure,
let's do it." And we started texting back

01:05:22.440 --> 01:05:25.450
and forth and "What do you want to call it?"
And I was like, "We should give, like, a poker

01:05:25.450 --> 01:05:29.359
thing since it's like poker. We should call
it All-In." And so the two of us started doing

01:05:29.359 --> 01:05:34.510
it, but this was during COVID. So then we're
like, "Well, who knows about COVID?" And Sacks

01:05:34.510 --> 01:05:38.059
had been doing a bunch of research on it,
and he bought ventilators and he had masks.

01:05:38.059 --> 01:05:41.119
Tim Ferriss: So explain who Sacks is.
Jason Calacanis: So David Sacks is an old

01:05:41.119 --> 01:05:47.279
friend of mine who worked at PayPal with Peter
Thiel, went to college with Peter Thiel, is

01:05:47.279 --> 01:05:55.700
a Republican, independent, really, chess club,
debate club, brilliant product manager, created

01:05:55.700 --> 01:05:59.490
Yammer, sold to Microsoft for a million dollars,
a billion dollars. Just really smart individual,

01:05:59.490 --> 01:06:04.579
but right-leaning. Seriously right-leaning,
like writing articles, donating to people

01:06:04.579 --> 01:06:09.260
on the right. And we became close friends.
Our families would go on vacation together,

01:06:09.260 --> 01:06:14.940
just as close of friends as you could be.
And then we had him on as a guest to talk

01:06:14.940 --> 01:06:19.760
about, "Hey, what's going on with this Wuhan
flu thing and what's going on with Trump?"

01:06:19.760 --> 01:06:22.020
And people were very scared and confused that
time.

01:06:22.020 --> 01:06:25.300
And then we had another friend who came to
poker. I didn't have a big relationship with

01:06:25.300 --> 01:06:29.820
Friedberg at the time, I just knew him from
poker. And David Friedberg, who runs something

01:06:29.820 --> 01:06:34.579
called the Production Board, he was an early
Google employee, came on and he actually created

01:06:34.579 --> 01:06:40.450
climate.com and sold that to Monsanto. So
he understands science at a very deep level.

01:06:40.450 --> 01:06:46.539
And so all of a sudden, this thing clicks,
and then Robinhood and that whole AMC thing

01:06:46.539 --> 01:06:50.280
happened, and I was an investor in Robinhood,
and we started talking about that. And the

01:06:50.280 --> 01:06:55.430
show just caught heat and the dynamic between
the four of us people really started to like,

01:06:55.430 --> 01:06:57.900
and people were —
Tim Ferriss: Caught heat in a good way.

01:06:57.900 --> 01:07:02.950
Jason Calacanis: I think caught heat in, like,
"Wow, this is really dynamic. They're smart,

01:07:02.950 --> 01:07:07.980
they're opinionated, and they're successful."
And so when people see that you've made a

01:07:07.980 --> 01:07:12.299
lot of money, is one thing I learned in my
life, it's like the day before the Weblogs,

01:07:12.299 --> 01:07:16.970
Inc. sale and then the day after, when I had
no money and then became a millionaire, people

01:07:16.970 --> 01:07:21.299
treated me incredibly different. All of a
sudden, I got 30 more IQ points and I was

01:07:21.299 --> 01:07:24.710
two feet taller. I was like, I'm the same
guy, but people would just take you a lot

01:07:24.710 --> 01:07:30.579
more seriously. And it just is a really good
thing to understand. Literally if you inherited

01:07:30.579 --> 01:07:35.059
a billion dollars, people would think you're
a genius. It's a big failing of human nature

01:07:35.059 --> 01:07:39.550
that we have this weird thing with the scorecard
that we think the scorecard of dollars is

01:07:39.550 --> 01:07:46.660
important. It's not. Long story short, all
the stuff comes together and it seems to connect

01:07:46.660 --> 01:07:53.580
with people and it starts racing up the charts.
And the last couple of weeks, I think we're

01:07:53.580 --> 01:08:00.970
in number 24, 26, 27, just top 30 podcasts
in the world. Gwyneth Paltrow wrote about

01:08:00.970 --> 01:08:06.141
it in her Goop newsletter a couple weeks ago,
that she's obsessed with the podcast, the

01:08:06.141 --> 01:08:10.750
personalities. And so This Week in Startups
had millions of listeners every month and

01:08:10.750 --> 01:08:14.109
doing incredible, five days a week, making
millions of dollars in advertising. But it's

01:08:14.109 --> 01:08:17.640
a very niche thing. It's just about startups.
So if you're in the startup space, you would

01:08:17.640 --> 01:08:22.000
want to take a selfie with me. All-In has
crossed over in a way you experience with

01:08:22.000 --> 01:08:26.140
the Tim Ferriss Podcast and people who are
pursuing excellence. It's a much wider aperture

01:08:26.140 --> 01:08:30.750
of people. And so this thing just appeals
to a lot of people and it's — literally,

01:08:30.750 --> 01:08:33.770
people say, it's like their Sunday morning
ritual, or their Saturday morning ritual.

01:08:33.770 --> 01:08:38.290
It's replaced the Sunday morning talk show.
And it turns out Politico is writing some

01:08:38.290 --> 01:08:42.941
kind of a profile of us right now, because
it's become so influential in Washington,

01:08:42.941 --> 01:08:47.000
because we talk about the economy, technology,
and technologies obviously have a big impact

01:08:47.000 --> 01:08:52.520
on the economy and politics, whatever. So
anyway, in some ways it's become a hit. It's

01:08:52.520 --> 01:08:59.420
very weird for me because I've had a couple
of hits before, but this one also is bigger

01:08:59.420 --> 01:09:03.890
and I'm not in control of it.
I have three partners and all three of my

01:09:03.890 --> 01:09:11.190
partners on it have never had a boss. So you've
literally got four of the most opinionated

01:09:11.190 --> 01:09:15.540
friends who've never had a boss who have four
different directions they want to take it.

01:09:15.540 --> 01:09:21.210
And it is a bit chaotic and I'm the producer
of it as well, and did all the work, came

01:09:21.210 --> 01:09:28.920
up with the format, et cetera. But objectively,
Chamath has a series of fans. Friedberg has

01:09:28.920 --> 01:09:36.170
the stans. Sacks has the whole right wing.
So it's like a super team has come together,

01:09:36.170 --> 01:09:40.600
and it's very hard to keep a super team together
for any period of time. And this thing has

01:09:40.600 --> 01:09:44.480
come off the rails famously two or three times.
Tim Ferriss: So could you walk us through

01:09:44.480 --> 01:09:48.760
your favorite of those three times and how
it was resolved?

01:09:48.760 --> 01:09:53.420
Jason Calacanis: Well, I wanted to do an event,
because, you mentioned, bro, I like events,

01:09:53.420 --> 01:09:59.250
and I was like, let's do this All-In Summit.
I'll do all the work and you guys show up.

01:09:59.250 --> 01:10:07.290
I have a production team and here's what it
will be. And one of the four of us was in

01:10:07.290 --> 01:10:11.370
a complete panic and complaining and arguing
incessantly because he thought I was going

01:10:11.370 --> 01:10:16.630
to ruin their reputation because what if it's
not great? The thing comes out of the gate,

01:10:16.630 --> 01:10:20.020
people are comparing it to the TED Conference,
saying this is better than ReCode, or better

01:10:20.020 --> 01:10:24.390
than TED, and it's the new TED, and whatever,
because we got the most incredible speakers

01:10:24.390 --> 01:10:30.810
in the world showed up for us, just everybody.
I invited you, you didn't make it, but I'll

01:10:30.810 --> 01:10:35.920
get you next year. I don't know if I can afford
your speaking fee, but —

01:10:35.920 --> 01:10:42.250
Tim Ferriss: If it makes you feel any better,
I remember this, and I have not done any speaking,

01:10:42.250 --> 01:10:49.271
except for South by Southwest, I want to say
in three or four years. But —

01:10:49.271 --> 01:10:50.710
Jason Calacanis: There'll be an opportunity.
I'll interview you. No, there'll be some opportunity

01:10:50.710 --> 01:10:51.710
where you'll —
Tim Ferriss: And I have —

01:10:51.710 --> 01:10:52.710
Jason Calacanis: There will be funding for
us to do it.

01:10:52.710 --> 01:10:57.820
Tim Ferriss: And in my own defense, I have
been to events of yours before. I have spoken

01:10:57.820 --> 01:11:00.360
with you at events. Yes. So I couldn't make
that one.

01:11:00.360 --> 01:11:02.020
Jason Calacanis: Couldn't make that one. Yeah,
it's true.

01:11:02.020 --> 01:11:03.820
Tim Ferriss: Anyway, where does the conflict
come in?

01:11:03.820 --> 01:11:07.710
Jason Calacanis: So the conflict came in that
I just told him like, "Hey listen, I'm working

01:11:07.710 --> 01:11:12.640
over here. "You need to shut the fuck up and
let me just do my work and stop being in a

01:11:12.640 --> 01:11:17.080
panic attack all the time." And he's like,
"My reputation. What if it's bad? We pay — people

01:11:17.080 --> 01:11:20.280
paid $7,500 a ticket." I'm like, "Listen,
people pay $7,500 a ticket for every event.

01:11:20.280 --> 01:11:26.290
It's not a big deal." And then I gave half
the tickets for scholarship for people — anyway,

01:11:26.290 --> 01:11:30.870
we had this back and forth, and then I told
the person, I was like, "Friedberg, if you

01:11:30.870 --> 01:11:34.190
don't want to be on the show and you're complaining
so much, I got Brad Gerstner over here.

01:11:34.190 --> 01:11:36.600
Tim Ferriss: I love how he was anonymous up
until right now.

01:11:36.600 --> 01:11:41.270
Jason Calacanis: Whatever. Anyway, the good
news is, Friedberg and I, after this blowout,

01:11:41.270 --> 01:11:44.880
like many friendships, we actually are really
enjoying our time together, but it was another

01:11:44.880 --> 01:11:48.260
one of these stab you in the back, stab you
in the face things. You know, got the quote

01:11:48.260 --> 01:11:52.020
from Doug. I said, "Listen, if you're not
enjoying your time on the All-In podcast and

01:11:52.020 --> 01:11:56.190
you don't want to do the event, why don't
you do 25 episodes a year? And I'll have Brad

01:11:56.190 --> 01:12:00.330
Gerstner, who sits in when one of us is out,
as like the fifth Beatle, I'll have him do

01:12:00.330 --> 01:12:03.980
the other 25." And we got into this big fight
and the whole thing's going to explode and

01:12:03.980 --> 01:12:07.449
then these two are going to do their own podcasts,
whatever.

01:12:07.449 --> 01:12:12.480
And then we all just sat down and we realized
the audience fucking loves this. We love it.

01:12:12.480 --> 01:12:16.739
Let's not do any events. Let's not do any
spin outs. People wanted to do a TV show with

01:12:16.739 --> 01:12:22.770
us, top networks, all this stuff. And you
understand how big the podcasting business

01:12:22.770 --> 01:12:27.050
now is better than anybody. You've got a top
50 podcast, these podcasts have become a very

01:12:27.050 --> 01:12:33.590
big business. On top of that, my biggest media
success to date with my three partners refuse

01:12:33.590 --> 01:12:35.801
to do ads.
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, yeah. This is one I was

01:12:35.801 --> 01:12:40.900
