01:26:32.330 --> 01:26:36.920
Because he's not excited to talk about politics,
because he doesn't like it. With Sacks, I've

01:26:36.920 --> 01:26:43.210
got a tiger, like a fierce tiger. And if I
throw a couple of chickens out there, he's

01:26:43.210 --> 01:26:47.011
going to maul them. But I know what they are.
So I'm like, "Okay, here you go. Supreme Court

01:26:47.011 --> 01:26:51.190
decision." Loses his mind.
"Oh, here you go. Biden said this and it's

01:26:51.190 --> 01:26:54.800
inconsistent." Or boom, "Hey, here's something
with Trump." He's going to just go crazy.

01:26:54.800 --> 01:26:58.410
So now you got your lion going crazy. You
got your science nerd coming out of his shell.

01:26:58.410 --> 01:27:03.270
And then with Chamath, it's more like an alley-oop.
Tim Ferriss: Sweaters.

01:27:03.270 --> 01:27:05.510
Jason Calacanis: I know — what's that?
Tim Ferriss: Sweaters.

01:27:05.510 --> 01:27:10.770
Jason Calacanis: Loro Piana. Conspicuous consumption.
No, it really is more about markets and startups.

01:27:10.770 --> 01:27:20.619
And for him, I like to say to him, "Can you
explain this financial topic and why people

01:27:20.619 --> 01:27:25.130
are talking about interest rates are bad for
tech companies? Why would the interest rate

01:27:25.130 --> 01:27:30.300
have any impact on that?" Now I know the answer
to that, but he can actually explain it better

01:27:30.300 --> 01:27:34.270
than anybody on the squad. So what I'm doing
there as the point guard is like I have a

01:27:34.270 --> 01:27:38.750
clear path to the basket, I can just lay it
in and do the layup, but I see him out the

01:27:38.750 --> 01:27:42.510
corner of my eye, and I'm like, you know what
a big the audience have a better experience,

01:27:42.510 --> 01:27:46.760
is if I pretend I'm going to do the layup,
and then from behind my back, I throw it up

01:27:46.760 --> 01:27:52.619
to him and he slam dunks it.
And so I've learned with each of them how

01:27:52.619 --> 01:28:01.030
to feature them and make them really their
best selves. And then behind the scenes, when

01:28:01.030 --> 01:28:05.230
you see the point guard in basketball take
somebody and they whisper in their ear something,

01:28:05.230 --> 01:28:07.980
I very quietly give them notes on their performance,
on how to do that —

01:28:07.980 --> 01:28:11.969
Tim Ferriss: And this is after each episode?
Jason Calacanis: After each show. Oh, I did

01:28:11.969 --> 01:28:16.949
it had to do it more earlier. And then sometimes
I'll do it in the group chat, if I think it's

01:28:16.949 --> 01:28:21.469
something everybody can benefit from. But
more often than not, I'm doing it quietly.

01:28:21.469 --> 01:28:27.140
And so I had to say to Friedberg, "Listen,
you know, don't say a lot about politics,

01:28:27.140 --> 01:28:32.219
but I think your opinion actually represents
a large portion of the audience. And although

01:28:32.219 --> 01:28:38.790
you hate it, I think you expressing a little
bit is going to be fine. And if you're not

01:28:38.790 --> 01:28:43.230
happy with how it comes out, and I think it's
putting you at risk, I will tell you, and

01:28:43.230 --> 01:28:48.140
we can cut it from the show." And once I gave
him that, sort of, I think a little bit of

01:28:48.140 --> 01:28:51.810
a clear lane and encouragement, you will hear
him talk a little bit more about politics,

01:28:51.810 --> 01:28:56.400
not to the extent Sacks does. And then I had
to tell Sacks, "Hey Sacks, when I ask you

01:28:56.400 --> 01:29:03.800
something or I challenge you, I'm not disagreeing
with you. I'm helping you clarify your position

01:29:03.800 --> 01:29:06.699
or helping the audience understand your position
more."

01:29:06.699 --> 01:29:11.869
Tim Ferriss: Very, very smart way to put it.
Jason Calacanis: "And you are attacking me,

01:29:11.869 --> 01:29:16.830
telling me I'm watching MSNBC and I get my
information from Rachel Maddow. I didn't ask

01:29:16.830 --> 01:29:27.450
you to explain…" One time, I said, "Just
to be clear, Putin invaded Ukraine, not Biden.

01:29:27.450 --> 01:29:32.369
Correct?" And he just went off or whatever.
And I said, "The reason I'm asking you that

01:29:32.369 --> 01:29:37.790
is because the audience thinks, and the feedback
online is, you're a Putin apologist. It would

01:29:37.790 --> 01:29:43.750
help your position if you said to the audience,
'Putin and Putin alone is responsible for

01:29:43.750 --> 01:29:54.719
invading Ukraine.' Now how we manage Putin
and his unjust immoral invasion is up to us.

01:29:54.719 --> 01:29:59.170
And that's what we can debate." But I'm trying
to save him from being misunderstood.

01:29:59.170 --> 01:30:06.300
Tim Ferriss: And so you're like one part moderator,
one part coach, one part comms director.

01:30:06.300 --> 01:30:10.110
Jason Calacanis: Well, in a way —
Tim Ferriss: I'm not saying that as a bad

01:30:10.110 --> 01:30:11.800
thing. It's just like —
Jason Calacanis: No, no, no, I think it's

01:30:11.800 --> 01:30:13.810
super active.
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, you're helping everybody.

01:30:13.810 --> 01:30:17.540
Jason Calacanis: Well what I realized was
like — listen, you and I have done hundreds

01:30:17.540 --> 01:30:20.650
of interviews with people. Great. It's awesome.
We're both interested in people and learning

01:30:20.650 --> 01:30:26.449
from them and having great conversations.
But this became something different for me,

01:30:26.449 --> 01:30:30.610
being the orchestrator of the action.
Tim Ferriss: Oh, it's a different skill, different

01:30:30.610 --> 01:30:32.360
skill.
Jason Calacanis: It was a totally different

01:30:32.360 --> 01:30:36.960
skill. And I had done these moderations before
at events, and I had sometimes done round

01:30:36.960 --> 01:30:40.369
tables, news round tables, but a lot of times,
I was one of the featured players, not the

01:30:40.369 --> 01:30:43.380
point guard.
Tim Ferriss: Can I give one example of why

01:30:43.380 --> 01:30:48.460
they're totally different? In a one-on-one
conversation, you can have a guest who monologues.

01:30:48.460 --> 01:30:54.110
If you have a group of people, that can be
a huge problem. How do you deal with that

01:30:54.110 --> 01:30:56.560
specifically?
Jason Calacanis: There's two techniques. Yeah,

01:30:56.560 --> 01:31:01.170
two techniques, because you and I like technique.
So the two techniques I have. One of them

01:31:01.170 --> 01:31:07.150
is I say to people beforehand, "Great job
on the show today. At this moment in time,

01:31:07.150 --> 01:31:11.940
you made four really great points. It would've
been better if you made your first one or

01:31:11.940 --> 01:31:17.750
two and then said, 'I'm really interested
in what Sacks thinks of that.' Or 'I'm curious,

01:31:17.750 --> 01:31:20.420
Sacks, what you think?' And then you have
those two. Just write them on a piece of paper

01:31:20.420 --> 01:31:25.280
there. There'll be time for those two. We'll
circle back around. But now it doesn't look

01:31:25.280 --> 01:31:30.320
like you are taking all the chips off the
table." Because in a conversation, with four

01:31:30.320 --> 01:31:35.840
smart people about a topic, there might be
five good points to make. All four people

01:31:35.840 --> 01:31:39.450
might understand all five points or four of
the points. And then if somebody comes in

01:31:39.450 --> 01:31:44.739
and they're just like, ding, ding, ding, ding,
I just took all the good points, there's nothing

01:31:44.739 --> 01:31:48.080
left for anybody else to do.
And so you want to share the ball a little

01:31:48.080 --> 01:31:51.170
bit. You took two good points. You let somebody
else make a good point. You could have made

01:31:51.170 --> 01:31:54.239
that point. It was an obvious one, but you
let somebody else have it. It's a little bit

01:31:54.239 --> 01:31:58.580
generous as a performer. So it'd be like,
you know, you've got some incredible guitar

01:31:58.580 --> 01:32:05.100
player. They come out and they just do a solo
of six minutes and you're like, "Piano guy

01:32:05.100 --> 01:32:08.330
could have done a solo for a minute. You could
have done two. We could've given the drummer

01:32:08.330 --> 01:32:12.880
a minute and they could've let the vocalist
lead off the song with an a capella moment,

01:32:12.880 --> 01:32:18.400
right?" And so I've really tried to be better
at it and specifically tried to be better

01:32:18.400 --> 01:32:23.980
at it, because people have been critical of
me for interrupting certain people. And so

01:32:23.980 --> 01:32:29.040
I had to actually adapt to that. And so, because
certain people, if you interrupt them, it's

01:32:29.040 --> 01:32:32.150
a trigger for them or it can break their train
of thought.

01:32:32.150 --> 01:32:34.010
Tim Ferriss: And I ran into this —
Jason Calacanis: That's where I came up with

01:32:34.010 --> 01:32:35.010
—
Tim Ferriss: I ran into this recently and

01:32:35.010 --> 01:32:37.090
I was like, I wondered, "How would Jason handle
this?" I was curious.

01:32:37.090 --> 01:32:41.330
Jason Calacanis: So I came up with another
one. I said, "I'm going to give you the circle."

01:32:41.330 --> 01:32:45.040
So if people aren't watching right now, I'm
just taking my index finger, and I'm just

01:32:45.040 --> 01:32:50.110
given the wrap up sign. But I'm also nodding.
So I'm saying, "Yeah, good stuff. I'm giving

01:32:50.110 --> 01:32:54.560
you the good stuff. But hey, let's wrap that
point." And then I'll just stop and they finish

01:32:54.560 --> 01:33:01.380
their thought. And the other thing I do is
I just use, "Okay." And I got that from Sam

01:33:01.380 --> 01:33:07.570
Harris. I was listening to Sam's podcast.
He gets people monologuing. These are some

01:33:07.570 --> 01:33:11.540
of the most brilliant academics in the world
on his show. And you'll see sometimes he wants

01:33:11.540 --> 01:33:17.560
to break in, but I think in academia, it's
even more difficult to do that.

01:33:17.560 --> 01:33:20.011
Tim Ferriss: Verboten. Yeah.
Jason Calacanis: Verboten. That's the word

01:33:20.011 --> 01:33:25.870
I was looking for. My dyslexia was killing
me on that one. Forberwritten? Verboten. You

01:33:25.870 --> 01:33:33.420
let people finish, but he'll say, "Okay."
And it's like, "Okay, I got it. Okay, you

01:33:33.420 --> 01:33:38.190
did it." And then the person's like, "Oh,
oh, Sam has something to add." And that's

01:33:38.190 --> 01:33:44.020
in a one on one situation. You get good by
studying. I study a lot of what you do and

01:33:44.020 --> 01:33:56.791
you have a way when you're asking a question
of slowing down and it's almost as if you're

01:33:56.791 --> 01:34:03.480
bringing us into your brain for the formation
of the question. And that was me imitating

01:34:03.480 --> 01:34:04.540
you.
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.

01:34:04.540 --> 01:34:09.380
Jason Calacanis: But, yeah. You do it, it's
like somebody at a dinner party who's talking

01:34:09.380 --> 01:34:13.790
in a lower voice and everybody leans in. I
know it's going to be a fucking important

01:34:13.790 --> 01:34:19.659
question when you do that, and I don't know
if it's unconscious or it's conscious or you

01:34:19.659 --> 01:34:28.610
are truly forming the question. But when listening
to Tim Ferriss, when Tim slows down and Tim

01:34:28.610 --> 01:34:38.619
is thinking, "I want to ask you one more question.
I have it on this note here. Hold on…" It

01:34:38.619 --> 01:34:43.270
means it's a really important question and
it really does work as a technique and everybody's

01:34:43.270 --> 01:34:47.970
techniques doesn't have to work for everybody.
Like I talk fast. You talk a little slower,

01:34:47.970 --> 01:34:53.210
more methodically. Sam talks even more monotone.
Tim Ferriss: He speaks in finished prose.

01:34:53.210 --> 01:34:59.139
Jason Calacanis: Unless it's about Trump,
then he gets on tilt. It is funny how we all

01:34:59.139 --> 01:35:02.350
study each other, right?
Tim Ferriss: Oh, yeah, I love doing it. I

01:35:02.350 --> 01:35:05.600
mean, I remember doing so much studying in
the beginning. I'm still studying.

01:35:05.600 --> 01:35:09.360
Jason Calacanis: Me too.
Tim Ferriss: It's fun to notice, because when

01:35:09.360 --> 01:35:14.860
you're in the game and you're doing a lot
of interviewing, I don't do as much moderating,

01:35:14.860 --> 01:35:19.850
so I can't speak to that, but you begin to
notice, just like you noticed, you begin to

01:35:19.850 --> 01:35:24.190
notice the little things that were you not
also doing a lot of reps you wouldn't pick

01:35:24.190 --> 01:35:26.510
up.
Jason Calacanis: It's about the reps.

01:35:26.510 --> 01:35:30.510
Tim Ferriss: I wanted to ask you about moderators.
You mentioned The McLaughlin Group earlier.

01:35:30.510 --> 01:35:33.730
Are there any that stand out as good moderators
to you?

01:35:33.730 --> 01:35:37.530
Jason Calacanis: There's not a big cohort
of moderators, because almost everything is

01:35:37.530 --> 01:35:40.970
— yeah. I mean, you might say, what's his
name from Politically Incorrect?

01:35:40.970 --> 01:35:45.520
Tim Ferriss: Bill Maher?
Jason Calacanis: Bill Maher. So Bill Maher,

01:35:45.520 --> 01:35:51.880
he can drop something funny into a moderated
bit. So two people are talking and I do a

01:35:51.880 --> 01:35:58.560
little comedy on the side and will sometimes
make jokes during the podcast. And I have

01:35:58.560 --> 01:36:03.510
seen him do this particularly well. He's watching
two people spar and he's kind of set that

01:36:03.510 --> 01:36:08.690
up and he's leaning back and then he just
lets a little funny thing out.

01:36:08.690 --> 01:36:14.000
So as a device for a moderator, it's getting
super heated and then you drop something funny

01:36:14.000 --> 01:36:18.260
and you'll see me do this on all end. It kind
of just cracks the whole thing open. Everybody's

01:36:18.260 --> 01:36:21.960
like, okay, that was getting really serious,
but we're all humans and we can laugh too

01:36:21.960 --> 01:36:26.460
about it. So somebody's having some crazy
debate about Roe v. Wade and then he makes

01:36:26.460 --> 01:36:31.020
some joke and you're just like, whoa, that's
a funny joke. Oh, I can laugh about Roe v.

01:36:31.020 --> 01:36:36.150
Wade and abortion? Oh my god, I'm not allowed
to laugh about that. That's a dicey place

01:36:36.150 --> 01:36:40.710
to be, but that comedic interruption, it works
pretty well, but there aren't many people

01:36:40.710 --> 01:36:42.270
or shows that do it well.
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.

01:36:42.270 --> 01:36:46.440
Jason Calacanis: I think you could pull it
off, I think with, you have your great rapport

01:36:46.440 --> 01:36:52.810
with Kevin Rose and if you've got one or two
more you could do your own little All-In/McLaughlin

01:36:52.810 --> 01:36:54.650
Group thing, it would be quite successful,
I think.

01:36:54.650 --> 01:36:58.280
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, I guess it comes down to
the chemistry, like you said, because with

01:36:58.280 --> 01:37:03.580
Kevin it's like we just, it's having a good
improv comedy partner or something. It's like

01:37:03.580 --> 01:37:08.929
your styles just work for some reason and
then you can always improve the craft and

01:37:08.929 --> 01:37:13.550
work on it. But there is just this baseline
of compatible chemistry.

01:37:13.550 --> 01:37:17.860
Jason Calacanis: When you look at your podcast
player. If I see Random Show, that's just

01:37:17.860 --> 01:37:22.469
like, I snap that immediately, when The Random
Show comes up in my feed. Because you know

01:37:22.469 --> 01:37:27.500
what? We were going back to friendship. We
spent a little time in this conversation.

01:37:27.500 --> 01:37:31.320
Friendship is so special. It is one of those
very special things in the world's unique.

01:37:31.320 --> 01:37:40.250
And when people see friendship, true friendship,
it's such an intoxicating thing to know that

01:37:40.250 --> 01:37:47.210
you and Kevin are having a great time catching
up and we get to be there for it.

01:37:47.210 --> 01:37:52.270
And I do think that is a big part of why people
have become addicted to All-In. And we had

01:37:52.270 --> 01:37:57.890
one or two weeks we took off and man, I have
never had people, I was so depressed. "God,

01:37:57.890 --> 01:38:01.660
what's going on?" And then when the show was
going to break up, and "Oh, my god, what's

01:38:01.660 --> 01:38:07.719
going on? This sucks, man." People wrote me
very long notes. "I really think you guys

01:38:07.719 --> 01:38:12.320
need to think about the fans and the impact
is going to have on us." And I feel like we're

01:38:12.320 --> 01:38:18.200
in Pink Floyd and Roger Waters and Gilmore
are doing separate tours and they're like,

01:38:18.200 --> 01:38:23.380
"It's the same songs, folks. You could just
listen to the old albums."

01:38:23.380 --> 01:38:28.801
Tim Ferriss: I think there's another piece
which is right parallel to this, and that

01:38:28.801 --> 01:38:36.000
is with friendship on display, you get to
hear people also talking as humans without

01:38:36.000 --> 01:38:41.310
overly self censoring in a world where that
has become the norm. Do you know what I mean?

01:38:41.310 --> 01:38:45.150
Jason Calacanis: Yeah, no, just now in our
conversation, when we were bringing up "Ouch!

01:38:45.150 --> 01:38:49.670
My Balls!" from Idiocracy, I was like, "Oh,
we're going there? Oh, no. Are we going to

01:38:49.670 --> 01:38:51.950
get canceled for liking a funny bit in the
movie?"

01:38:51.950 --> 01:38:58.770
Tim Ferriss: Yeah. And the fact of the matter
is as, I don't think anyone will disagree.

01:38:58.770 --> 01:39:04.540
If anyone listening to this podcast had their
group chats publicly shared, everything would

01:39:04.540 --> 01:39:10.849
be over. It's just like their chats with their
five closest friends. All of us would be unmasked

01:39:10.849 --> 01:39:14.650
for the complete children that we all are.
Jason Calacanis: Yes. Goofballs.

01:39:14.650 --> 01:39:19.869
Tim Ferriss: And despite that, given the times,
I think the majority of folks feel like they

01:39:19.869 --> 01:39:28.280
have to put on a mask and stuff all of that
so far down, because the risk seems so high.

01:39:28.280 --> 01:39:33.409
So when you have a show where friends are
having fun, they're not taking themselves

01:39:33.409 --> 01:39:39.010
or anything too seriously, even though they
do take things seriously in life. It allows

01:39:39.010 --> 01:39:41.910
everyone to just exhale for a second.
Jason Calacanis: Yeah. Chill out.

01:39:41.910 --> 01:39:46.040
Tim Ferriss: Take off the psychological corset
that they strain to put on in the morning.

01:39:46.040 --> 01:39:51.869
Jason Calacanis: It is very weird being a
Gen Xer where the Overton window was so much

01:39:51.869 --> 01:39:57.260
wider when we were young and now the Overton
window, people are like, close it tighter,

01:39:57.260 --> 01:40:02.810
close it tighter. And I'm like, who cares?
Just a window. You can pick how wide or open

01:40:02.810 --> 01:40:09.659
you want it to be, but people are really trying
to narrow the topics you can discuss in a

01:40:09.659 --> 01:40:15.870
way that I find kind of a bummer, because
all those conversations are still going to

01:40:15.870 --> 01:40:19.960
occur. They're just going to occur in private.
I almost would rather they be occurring publicly

01:40:19.960 --> 01:40:22.440
so we could make more progress as a society.
It feels like —

01:40:22.440 --> 01:40:24.290
Tim Ferriss: Yeah. I agree.
Jason Calacanis: It feels like it's going

01:40:24.290 --> 01:40:28.110
to work against us. I hope it opens up. That's
why I was talking about that Red Scare podcast

01:40:28.110 --> 01:40:36.140
or another podcast. I like is the guy who
wrote Less Than Zero and American Psycho,

01:40:36.140 --> 01:40:37.730
Bret Easton Ellis.
Tim Ferriss: Oh yeah.

01:40:37.730 --> 01:40:42.770
Jason Calacanis: And both of these are on
Patreon and both of these are behind paywalls,

01:40:42.770 --> 01:40:50.280
which I think is interesting. Both of them
are podcasts where people are being an Overton

01:40:50.280 --> 01:40:54.960
window that's twice as wide open.
Tim Ferriss: But just for folks who are listening:

01:40:54.960 --> 01:40:58.659
Overton window?
Jason Calacanis: What's allowable to discuss

01:40:58.659 --> 01:41:05.690
in society writ large. I'm using a fancy word.
What you're allowed to talk about in society

01:41:05.690 --> 01:41:08.219
without feeling ashamed or that you are being
inappropriate. So there was a time —

01:41:08.219 --> 01:41:12.921
Tim Ferriss: Or you're going to be persecuted.
Jason Calacanis: Persecuted for sure. So there

01:41:12.921 --> 01:41:17.550
was a time when just even talking about somebody
being a homosexual was outside the Overton

01:41:17.550 --> 01:41:21.040
window. And if you brought it up, everybody
would leave the dinner party, be aghast. "Oh,

01:41:21.040 --> 01:41:25.380
my God." So you had to just treat a person
who was a gay person at a dinner party as

01:41:25.380 --> 01:41:30.110
like, "Oh, yes, they've got a roommate," and
we're just going to sweep that under the rug.

01:41:30.110 --> 01:41:34.210
"Oh, yeah, they're here with their roommate.
They split their rent, because it's cheaper."

01:41:34.210 --> 01:41:40.400
And then you could be like, "Yeah, Ernie and
Bert are gay. They're roommates. Pretty obvious."

01:41:40.400 --> 01:41:45.440
Tim Ferriss: I like the little judo move you
did with the Ernie and Bert there, the jelly

01:41:45.440 --> 01:41:48.909
and peanut butter. I was like, "Well, wait
a second. Oh, wow." I'm just, "Now I know."

01:41:48.909 --> 01:41:50.790
Yeah. I like it. I like it.
Jason Calacanis: Yeah.

01:41:50.790 --> 01:41:57.650
Tim Ferriss: So you have this experience with
these friends, you build this podcast. Two

01:41:57.650 --> 01:42:02.460
questions that you can pick, either or both.
The first is, you mentioned capturing lightning

01:42:02.460 --> 01:42:08.420
in a bottle. And I'd be curious how you would
explain the success of this show, because

01:42:08.420 --> 01:42:14.440
there must be, you were so deliberate, so
deliberate. Every example you've given, I

01:42:14.440 --> 01:42:22.540
think from at least your earlier career, shows
how systematic and methodical you are. So

01:42:22.540 --> 01:42:26.850
I know it wasn't an accident. It may have
coalesced in a way that couldn't have been

01:42:26.850 --> 01:42:31.480
predicted, but I don't think it was an accident.
So how would you explain the popularity? What

01:42:31.480 --> 01:42:32.610
are the ingredients?
Jason Calacanis: Yeah.

01:42:32.610 --> 01:42:39.020
Tim Ferriss: And then secondly, how do you
now think about success for yourself? And

01:42:39.020 --> 01:42:43.900
you made that joke earlier about being separated
from the private jet, your friends standing

01:42:43.900 --> 01:42:45.360
in the way of this private jet. So I was just
curious —

01:42:45.360 --> 01:42:48.380
Jason Calacanis: Jet blockers.
Tim Ferriss: Yeah. How do you think about

01:42:48.380 --> 01:42:53.290
success and or power? Because that played
such a role in your life up to this point.

01:42:53.290 --> 01:42:58.650
So the ingredients that made the podcast successful
or that make it successful, and then the success

01:42:58.650 --> 01:43:00.840
power piece.
Jason Calacanis: I think there is something

01:43:00.840 --> 01:43:06.950
that comes from repetition and getting reps
in, and you and I are both big fans of that.

01:43:06.950 --> 01:43:11.420
And so I think having done whatever at the
time, maybe 13- or 1,400 episodes of This

01:43:11.420 --> 01:43:18.780
Week in Startups and all those event interviews,
I came into All-In with a 10-person podcasting

01:43:18.780 --> 01:43:23.920
team. I mean, that includes some salespeople,
so whatever. But having had done so many podcasts

01:43:23.920 --> 01:43:30.931
that I came in with so much knowledge and
ability and it was like, oh, my skill, but

01:43:30.931 --> 01:43:34.469
a different playing field.
It would be like somebody who plays ping pong

01:43:34.469 --> 01:43:37.430
all of a sudden gets on a tennis court, which
happened to me. I used to play ping pong as

01:43:37.430 --> 01:43:41.770
a kid and I bought a house that has a tennis
court, quite embarrassingly. It's a mind-blowing

01:43:41.770 --> 01:43:46.030
thing for a kid from Brooklyn to have a tennis
court on their property. You're like, "Wait,

01:43:46.030 --> 01:43:51.830
aren't there two for 10,000 people in Brooklyn?
And you have to wait to get on it." And then

01:43:51.830 --> 01:43:57.440
I have one, where I can just sit there and
let it go unused for a month. Like ping pong

01:43:57.440 --> 01:44:02.960
and tennis is the same thing. There's a net,
there's a ball, you put spin on it. And I

01:44:02.960 --> 01:44:07.820
started taking tennis lessons this year and
it was like, woo, your brain kind of lights

01:44:07.820 --> 01:44:08.860
up?
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.

01:44:08.860 --> 01:44:13.739
Jason Calacanis: Same thing, but different
game skill. So I think it was all that preparation

01:44:13.739 --> 01:44:18.030
had led to this new thing that you actually
cued off on and we talked about last year

01:44:18.030 --> 01:44:22.400
for an hour during the winter of, "Hey, what
happened there?" And so I think that was the

01:44:22.400 --> 01:44:27.460
lightning in the bottle for me personally,
was maybe I'm a better moderator than an interviewer.

01:44:27.460 --> 01:44:30.420
Maybe that actually was sitting there the
whole time. Maybe I'm a better tennis player

01:44:30.420 --> 01:44:34.139
than a ping pong player.
So that for me, I think has opened up a lot

01:44:34.139 --> 01:44:39.300
of possibility, possibilities of what I could
do with the last 10, 20, 30, 40 years, whatever

01:44:39.300 --> 01:44:44.739
I've got left on this planet, before the tears
in the rain moment happens. And so it has

01:44:44.739 --> 01:44:47.670
opened my aperture a bit and I am thinking
about things differently, which dovetails

01:44:47.670 --> 01:44:54.510
with your second question, which is, after
I made whatever amount of millions of dollars,

01:44:54.510 --> 01:45:00.660
I had a profound realization when I go out
to eat with a friend who's a billionaire of

01:45:00.660 --> 01:45:05.010
me, you, and Travis were sitting around, or
me, you, and Matt Mullenweg were sitting around

01:45:05.010 --> 01:45:09.560
and we took out our net worths and was like,
"Oh, my God, you and I are down here. These

01:45:09.560 --> 01:45:14.700
two guys are up here."
We all go and have a steak. Steak tastes the

01:45:14.700 --> 01:45:22.920
same, we all go and go skiing or we go to
Japan. Same experience. And once I realized

01:45:22.920 --> 01:45:28.980
that, I was like, for me, it might be different
for other people, the scorecard of cash doesn't

01:45:28.980 --> 01:45:34.440
matter beyond a certain point. And for me
it was the freedom to do whatever I want in

01:45:34.440 --> 01:45:38.150
my life and to take care of my three daughters,
take care of my wife, and be a good provider,

01:45:38.150 --> 01:45:41.080
take care of my extended family if they needed
some help on the margins.

01:45:41.080 --> 01:45:48.310
And once that happened, I was like, I actually,
I drive a Model Y and I prefer that to the

01:45:48.310 --> 01:45:52.690
Plaid. And I have a Model X, I enjoy sometimes
the New York strip better than Wagyu. I just

01:45:52.690 --> 01:45:54.739
kind of like the flavor of it. It's a fifth
of the price.

01:45:54.739 --> 01:45:59.219
So once you start to realize, okay, these
are just constructs and money is just not

01:45:59.219 --> 01:46:05.210
the right scorecard. The right scorecard is,
did you wake up and couldn't wait to get out

01:46:05.210 --> 01:46:09.200
of bed. To me, that's the true scorecard.
I cannot wait to get on air and do this weekend

01:46:09.200 --> 01:46:14.440
startups with Molly and talk about tech. Can't
wait to meet with founders. I can't wait to

01:46:14.440 --> 01:46:18.990
teach another course on how to start a company.
Can't wait to see my friends for dinner, can't

01:46:18.990 --> 01:46:22.750
wait to help my friends if they're in need.
Can't wait to come on the Tim Ferriss pod.

01:46:22.750 --> 01:46:27.320
If I can't wait to do the activity, I know
I'm doing something right. When you invited

01:46:27.320 --> 01:46:31.790
me on, I went to Jade, I was like, my wife's
name is Jade, and I was like, "Oh, Tim invited

01:46:31.790 --> 01:46:37.800
me on the podcast. I can't wait to do it."
And I have never, that very rarely happens,

01:46:37.800 --> 01:46:41.909
but I was like, "I listen to the Tim Ferriss
podcasts all the time, and I'm sure at some

01:46:41.909 --> 01:46:47.659
point I'll be on it." But the act of doing
it with you, to me was like, "Oh, I'm excited."

01:46:47.659 --> 01:46:53.930
And being excited and enthusiastic, to me,
it just means something has aligned. And when

01:46:53.930 --> 01:46:56.630
I'm writing, you and I have the same thing,
when you get, I don't know where it is for

01:46:56.630 --> 01:47:02.130
you, but somewhere around a third of the book,
things start heating up for me and all of

01:47:02.130 --> 01:47:08.820
a sudden the first third of the book is like,
"Oh." And then all of a sudden, "Oh, I know

01:47:08.820 --> 01:47:13.199
what sections I have to do. I can see behind
the next corner, the book just flows." And

01:47:13.199 --> 01:47:16.469
you're like, "Boom," 2,000 words are coming
out at a clip.

01:47:16.469 --> 01:47:22.110
And those kind of moments, when you have that
flow and you have that enthusiasm for life,

01:47:22.110 --> 01:47:26.520
you have to construct a life around those.
And then life is just pulling you out of bed

01:47:26.520 --> 01:47:31.220
every day and just telling you, I got to go
to bed every night. I go to bed. And I'm just

01:47:31.220 --> 01:47:36.150
like, I don't want to close my eyes. I want
to just do another hour. And it's hard for

01:47:36.150 --> 01:47:41.760
me to go to bed some nights, because my enthusiasm
is so high for life and I wasn't always that

01:47:41.760 --> 01:47:44.650
way. A lot of it was a struggle. A lot of
it was trying to figure out who I was.

01:47:44.650 --> 01:47:51.320
But I really do want to enjoy this last couple
of decades of if there's an interesting project

01:47:51.320 --> 01:47:54.690
or moment that's going to create that great
memory and going to make me enthusiastic,

01:47:54.690 --> 01:48:00.739
I want to do it. And if that means helping
out a friend on a special project or going

01:48:00.739 --> 01:48:07.170
on a friend's podcast or whatever, doing a
new podcast. I feel very blessed that I got

01:48:07.170 --> 01:48:13.909
here and I want to just squeeze every single
drop of juice out of that box of oranges.

01:48:13.909 --> 01:48:20.800
I mean, really think about it, our friend
Dave Goldberg, rest in peace, Tony Hsieh,

01:48:20.800 --> 01:48:26.560
the darkness there, it can be over and it
can be, it's exactly like our age range where

01:48:26.560 --> 01:48:31.860
we start saying goodbye to people. And so
if you are in your 20s or 30s, you're just

01:48:31.860 --> 01:48:37.110
on adrenaline and then you get to 40s and
50s and you're like, "Huh, what do I do with

01:48:37.110 --> 01:48:42.040
what's left?" Especially if some things have
hit for you. If you're Tim Ferriss, you've

01:48:42.040 --> 01:48:46.130
got to really start thinking, "Okay, well
I'm one of the top podcasters in the world,

01:48:46.130 --> 01:48:49.599
one of the top authors in the world, is this
actually making me happy?"

01:48:49.599 --> 01:48:52.480
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.
Jason Calacanis: And that's the thing I'm

01:48:52.480 --> 01:48:57.560
writing in my new book about is, my new book
is about money and wealth and I really —

01:48:57.560 --> 01:48:58.830
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, I can't wait to read it.
Jason Calacanis: Can't wait, well I want to

01:48:58.830 --> 01:49:02.040
send you some early stuff and get your thoughts
on it, but I'm writing about this paradox

01:49:02.040 --> 01:49:05.780
that you and I are talking about right now,
which is what happens when you catch the car?

01:49:05.780 --> 01:49:08.630
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.
Jason Calacanis: What happens? It's part,

01:49:08.630 --> 01:49:13.949
how do you catch the car? But then once I
get you on the hook for that, how do you get

01:49:13.949 --> 01:49:19.690
the money? Now what happens when you have
caught the car? And then now you have to look

01:49:19.690 --> 01:49:26.500
in the mirror, "I caught the car. Okay, the
adrenaline rush is over. Am I actually happy?

01:49:26.500 --> 01:49:30.550
Did I want to catch that car? Did I want to
be Tim Ferriss? Did I want to be J Cal? Did

01:49:30.550 --> 01:49:32.670
I want to be Travis? Did I want to be Matt
Mullenweg?"

01:49:32.670 --> 01:49:34.650
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.
Jason Calacanis: "Is this actually making

01:49:34.650 --> 01:49:42.699
me happy? What now?" And that's where Buddhism
and a lot of soulfulness has hit me in my

01:49:42.699 --> 01:49:46.690
50s or right before my 40s, basically when
my two friends died. Two of my poker buddies

01:49:46.690 --> 01:49:53.060
died and it just reprogrammed my brain in
a major way, those two events. And it's actually

01:49:53.060 --> 01:49:58.500
been a gift in a way, because now I just look
at the world differently. It's a big —

01:49:58.500 --> 01:50:00.830
Tim Ferriss: Put things in perspective.
Jason Calacanis: Yeah. I mean it does really.

01:50:00.830 --> 01:50:05.060
Tim Ferriss: And another pattern I've noticed,
just with respect to catching the car, is

01:50:05.060 --> 01:50:17.349
that not everyone, but a lot of achievers
I know who have been driven enough off and

01:50:17.349 --> 01:50:23.660
worked hard enough and focused long enough
and sacrificed enough to make a lot of money,

01:50:23.660 --> 01:50:29.110
when they finally crossed that threshold where
they can't really rationalize the need to

01:50:29.110 --> 01:50:34.720
make more, which by the way, I mean, not to
plug my own, I haven't done this, I haven't

01:50:34.720 --> 01:50:39.310
mentioned The 4-Hour Workweek in a long time,
but if you actually read some of the basics,

01:50:39.310 --> 01:50:45.940
the fundamentals in that book, it outlines
just how much self-deception goes into believing

01:50:45.940 --> 01:50:49.860
that you need 20, 30, 50, a 100 million dollars.
It's mostly unjustifiable.

01:50:49.860 --> 01:50:55.980
Jason Calacanis: Totally.
Tim Ferriss: And when you get enough money

01:50:55.980 --> 01:51:01.360
that you've won the game, you always thought
would answer your prayers and remove all the

01:51:01.360 --> 01:51:07.510
pain, anxiety, and depression, and it doesn't,
that is a terrifying moment.

01:51:07.510 --> 01:51:12.810
Jason Calacanis: Yeah.
Tim Ferriss: Oh, my God, do people — and

01:51:12.810 --> 01:51:14.901
I'll include myself in this group of people
—

01:51:14.901 --> 01:51:17.400
Jason Calacanis: Yeah.
Tim Ferriss: — begin to panic, because you're

01:51:17.400 --> 01:51:20.340
like, "Wait a second, if that didn't fix these
things…"

01:51:20.340 --> 01:51:24.080
Jason Calacanis: Yep. "What will?"
Tim Ferriss: "What in the fuck am I supposed

01:51:24.080 --> 01:51:29.790
to do?" And it can be, and I don't want to
make it a complete swan song. I mean, look,

01:51:29.790 --> 01:51:33.770
it's better to — all things being equal
— to have the money than to not have the

01:51:33.770 --> 01:51:44.710
money. However, the prevalence of depression
in successful people, that has increased,

01:51:44.710 --> 01:51:51.030
rather than decreased, after they catch the
car, it's worth studying. I think it tells

01:51:51.030 --> 01:51:54.920
us a lot about the human condition, even if
you never end up catching the car.

01:51:54.920 --> 01:51:59.360
Jason Calacanis: And this is where doing the
work internally, and I really respect the

01:51:59.360 --> 01:52:05.969
fact that you've used some of the resources
you've gathered to do the work you're doing

01:52:05.969 --> 01:52:07.160
at psychedelics.
Tim Ferriss: Thank you.

01:52:07.160 --> 01:52:10.630
Jason Calacanis: I've never publicly talked
about the psychedelic work or psychedelics

01:52:10.630 --> 01:52:16.610
period, because as a person who sometimes
people follow what I do with a fan base — I

01:52:16.610 --> 01:52:22.150
talked about actually taking Ozempic for the
first time. I think there's suffering in the

01:52:22.150 --> 01:52:28.579
world that psychedelics are uniquely qualified,
I suspect. And you'd want to talk to a doctor

01:52:28.579 --> 01:52:33.270
and a therapist and do these things in a very
considered fashion. But the fact that you

01:52:33.270 --> 01:52:38.190
are looking at psilocybin and other people
are looking at ayahuasca or ketamine or whatever,

01:52:38.190 --> 01:52:41.780
I think the research and the suffering that's
happening, a lot of it is because people have

01:52:41.780 --> 01:52:48.010
not done enough of the work to look inside
and find out where that trauma is and maybe

01:52:48.010 --> 01:52:54.280
try and clear it and maybe try to understand
it. And that is, I think, the breakthrough

01:52:54.280 --> 01:52:58.750
that's going to happen in our lifetime, because
of people like you and some other friends

01:52:58.750 --> 01:53:04.840
of ours, who have dedicated significant resources
towards the study of these. I don't know if

01:53:04.840 --> 01:53:07.360
you saw Colorado? I think past —
Tim Ferriss: I did, yeah.

01:53:07.360 --> 01:53:10.280
Jason Calacanis: 122 yesterday.
Tim Ferriss: Yeah. It's big news. Very big

01:53:10.280 --> 01:53:11.800
news.
Jason Calacanis: It's big news. Colorado's

01:53:11.800 --> 01:53:17.920
going to allow therapists to do all these
different psychedelics in really safe settings

01:53:17.920 --> 01:53:22.400
with really qualified people for people who
have PTSD, people who have trauma, people

01:53:22.400 --> 01:53:27.699
who have anxiety, people who've been to wars,
people whose lives have been wars, and it's

01:53:27.699 --> 01:53:33.030
having a profound impact. And people are out
there and they're suffering.

01:53:33.030 --> 01:53:40.440
I really, really encourage them to go to therapy
and to maybe even research this stuff, because

01:53:40.440 --> 01:53:44.880
again, you give these disclaimers all the
time on this podcast, but there is help out

01:53:44.880 --> 01:53:50.480
there and you don't need to suffer alone.
And sometimes people will mask their suffering

01:53:50.480 --> 01:53:54.909
and mask their problems with performance.
And this is the show about performance and

01:53:54.909 --> 01:53:58.150
that's why people listen to it and people
are drawn to it. But sometimes we're drawn

01:53:58.150 --> 01:54:03.820
to performance, because we don't want to face
the pain or suffering that is driving that

01:54:03.820 --> 01:54:08.780
performance. The fuel, a lot of times those
hot coals that are burning, that's a lot of

01:54:08.780 --> 01:54:15.679
pain and suffering and it fuels an engine,
a mighty engine. But then you get to the destination

01:54:15.679 --> 01:54:21.290
we're talking about here and then, hey, maybe
this isn't serving me well anymore. And you

01:54:21.290 --> 01:54:24.310
want it to serve you well.
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, agreed. And I'll make just

01:54:24.310 --> 01:54:33.450
a couple recommendations for folks. One, this
is an extremely heavy podcast in terms of

01:54:33.450 --> 01:54:39.600
content, but if people are curious in the
capacity of researching psychedelic therapies

01:54:39.600 --> 01:54:45.330
or psychedelic assisted therapies, but also
looking at alternatives to that, I did do

01:54:45.330 --> 01:54:51.199
a podcast that people can find at tim.blog/trauma,
which is a conversation between myself and

01:54:51.199 --> 01:54:52.540
Debbie Millman.
Jason Calacanis: That's incredible.

01:54:52.540 --> 01:54:59.080
Tim Ferriss: Who suffered from a horrible
childhood abuse. And we took very different

01:54:59.080 --> 01:55:05.400
paths, but found solace, found support in
very, very different ways. And there are a

01:55:05.400 --> 01:55:10.110
lot of resources associated with the blog
post and the show notes that people can look

01:55:10.110 --> 01:55:14.860
at the other, if people are interested in
identifying at least some of the credible

01:55:14.860 --> 01:55:21.920
institutions and scientists doing research
on, say, complex PTSD and MDMA-assisted psychotherapy,

01:55:21.920 --> 01:55:28.920
which is now, I believe, through phase three
and psilocybin for treatment-resistant depression,

01:55:28.920 --> 01:55:34.140
et cetera. If you go to saiseifoundation.org,
that's the foundation I started some time

01:55:34.140 --> 01:55:40.060
ago, which is S, A, I, S, E, I, which means
"rebirth" in Japanese, saiseifoundation.org.

01:55:40.060 --> 01:55:44.079
There's a projects page where you can see
the various universities and a lot of the

01:55:44.079 --> 01:55:45.830
researchers.
Jason Calacanis: I've been looking for this

01:55:45.830 --> 01:55:49.480
to see where all the research is actually
occurring. You actually made a page. I was

01:55:49.480 --> 01:55:52.639
telling somebody recently, there should just
be a place people could go to see where the

01:55:52.639 --> 01:55:56.880
progress has been, because people are not
aware of how much people, I think it's next

01:55:56.880 --> 01:56:03.620
year that MDMA will be available in therapy
in some psychiatric settings, right?

01:56:03.620 --> 01:56:09.830
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, I'd have to check the latest
and greatest. It's certainly last time I checked

01:56:09.830 --> 01:56:15.760
in, it would be as potentially as soon as
the end of, say, 2023 or early 2024. I'll

01:56:15.760 --> 01:56:23.000
have to check. There's so much flux, because
there's not only the federal landscape and

01:56:23.000 --> 01:56:29.781
potential reclassification, which you can
do through demonstrating medical need. Because

01:56:29.781 --> 01:56:34.210
schedule one is supposed to be, and I'm painting
with a very simplistic brush here, but schedule

01:56:34.210 --> 01:56:40.390
one compounds are supposed to be drugs with
a high potential for abuse that have no known

01:56:40.390 --> 01:56:45.110
medical application. And rather than fighting
the political fight, which you do have to

01:56:45.110 --> 01:56:52.030
fight at some point, you can demonstrate medical
application through good science and clinical

01:56:52.030 --> 01:56:57.570
trials, which has been the area where I've
predominantly focused. But then you have Colorado,

01:56:57.570 --> 01:57:05.070
you have Oregon as well. And these parallel
systems or initiatives to create parallel

01:57:05.070 --> 01:57:12.480
systems on a state basis, a statewide basis,
that will be very interesting experiments.

01:57:12.480 --> 01:57:17.460
And they could go a lot of different ways.
I mean, it could all go well. There will probably

01:57:17.460 --> 01:57:24.630
be, as happens when therapies scale, various
issues and problems and scandals and so on.

01:57:24.630 --> 01:57:28.460
I expect that. Humans are humans, so they're
going to find a way to screw it up one way

01:57:28.460 --> 01:57:35.380
or the other, but net the hope, and I do think
there's a lot of good that has come of, say,

01:57:35.380 --> 01:57:41.710
the decriminalization nature initiatives,
although I disagree with some of their stances.

01:57:41.710 --> 01:57:48.320
It's a very exciting time of flux at the moment.
And as you put it, there is help out there

01:57:48.320 --> 01:57:50.801
and there are sources.
Jason Calacanis: If you think about what we

01:57:50.801 --> 01:57:55.849
saw happen with cannabis in this country and
the legalization of it, life went on. But

01:57:55.849 --> 01:58:00.219
these drugs are really going to help people,
I think, especially with the complex PTSD,

01:58:00.219 --> 01:58:02.000
especially with anxiety.
Tim Ferriss: Absolutely.

01:58:02.000 --> 01:58:04.940
Jason Calacanis: It's just great when combined
with a proper therapist, I think.

01:58:04.940 --> 01:58:09.219
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, they're very powerful,
as a friend of mine, I don't think he said

01:58:09.219 --> 01:58:15.070
it publicly, so I'll keep it anonymous, but
a very senior scientist. He said when you're

01:58:15.070 --> 01:58:20.630
working with psychedelic compounds, you are
working with nuclear power, so you just need

01:58:20.630 --> 01:58:26.510
have the right therapeutic context around
it. But they're very, very, very powerful.

01:58:26.510 --> 01:58:27.510
J Cal?
Jason Calacanis: Yeah?

01:58:27.510 --> 01:58:29.674
Tim Ferriss: Yeah. We're coming up on —
Jason Calacanis: I was like, oh wow. Just

01:58:29.674 --> 01:58:30.830
a quick 75 minutes, 90 minutes. I looked at
two hours.

01:58:30.830 --> 01:58:33.560
Tim Ferriss: Two hours.
Jason Calacanis: We talked two hours.

